INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(1277,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3865393','Return of whole-genome sequencing results in paediatric research: a statement of the P3G international paediatrics platform.','Knoppers BM, Avard D, Sénécal K, Zawati MH; P3G International Paediatrics Platform Members.','2013','derogation outweighing reaffirmed conceded xtending recogniz fter outweighs esearchers unsolicited binning regroups p3g accidentally penetrant purview tff forge micyrn rmga fonds frsq annelien bredenoord kristien hens wim pinxten leicester zawati p3g annelien bredenoord kristien hens wim pinxten soden 154ra135 saal pch bredenoord delden nrg3459 noor giesbertz bredenoord delden strasbourg strasbourg hens micyrn antommaria medforthcoming2013 anastova mahalatchimy rial sebbag rioux zawati pme avard senecal madadi sinnett','Whole-genome sequencing (WGS) reveals the genome of an individual including both rare mutations and genes that have a role in the expression of common medical conditions. The rapid development of WGS has substantially reduced both the cost and the time required to sequence an entire human genome. The availability of WGS is likely to change the current practice of both paediatric medicine and research by facilitating more accurate, sophisticated and cost-effective genetic testing. 1 WGS also reveals findings with clinical significance that are not within the scope of the original paediatric research objectives. This raises the ethical question of whether to return such WGS findings. Our purpose in this statement is to examine how to respect the ‘best interests of the child'' – ‘a primary consideration'' 2 – as well as the right of the child to voice his or her opinion 3 in the particular context of paediatric research using WGS. In the text below, we will use the term WGS results to include both research results per se (ie within the objectives of the project) and ‘incidental'' findings that are revealed via WGS. Our background reflections cover both existing and emerging guidance, approaches for the testing of adults, and relevant laws, before we propose our recommendations. Existing guidance General criteria At a general level, publically accessible lay summaries of general research results for research participants are increasingly the norm. More specifically, when the research project involves young children, these general results should be offered to the parents or guardians. When the research project involves adolescents, general results should also be offered to them in a manner appropriate to their level of development, comprehension and maturity. Communication of all individual WGS results, however, including those revealing possible adult predisposition or those of uncertain clinical significance, could lead to the over-prescribing of tests and create undue parental anxiety. There are also potential psychological harms such as alteration of self-image, distortion of parental perception of the child, increased anxiety and guilt, familial stress related to the identification of other at-risk family members, difficulty obtaining life and/or disability insurance, and the detection of non-paternity. 4 , 5 Yet, WGS may well reveal scientifically validated results with clinical utility that are medically actionable during childhood and thus would benefit the individual child. In all situations, and taking the specific context into account, the child''s best interests should guide decision making. 6 The release of WGS information is also complicated by the fact that children mature as they grow older and thus become more autonomous in their decision-making abilities. The mechanism for recognizing this in the research context is requiring assent from the child according to the degree and level of his or her maturity. 7 This is how the child can be involved and engaged in the decision-making process. 8 , 9 Generally, professional guidance on the communication of genetic information has favoured waiting until the child is capable of understanding its nature and consequences, including familial consequences. Accordingly, at maturity, a child could decide to undergo genetic testing or not. But today, with the arrival of WGS, we are faced with the issues of which WGS results should be communicated (or not) before the child reaches the age of majority as well as the familial consequences. Specific criteria The 2005 European Additional Protocol to the Convention on Human Rights and Biomedicine, concerning Biomedical Research 10 states that a condition for undertaking research with a child unable to consent is that ‘the results of the research have the potential to produce real and direct benefit to his or her health…[unless]…the ultimate attainment of results…[confers] benefit…to other persons in the same age category or afflicted with the same disease or disorder'' (art. 15). The latter ‘benefit to others'' serves as an exemption to the direct benefit requirement. Nevertheless, even if the research meets the ‘benefit to others'' requirement, it still must present only a minimal risk and a minimal burden to the child participant. This is important because the Additional Protocol targets clinical research, where as in the genetic research context, the nature of the risks and burdens are different. Indeed, for genetic testing for health purposes, it is instructive to note that this accepted research exemption to the direct benefit criterion is absent. Article 10 of the 2008 Additional Protocol Concerning Genetic Testing for Health Purposes 11 mandates that ‘a genetic test on a person who does not have the capacity to consent may only be carried out for his or her direct benefit…[and] where, according to law, a minor does not have the capacity to consent, a genetic test on this person shall be deferred until attainment of such capacity unless that delay would be detrimental to his or her health or well-being.'' Further, consistent with the recognition of both the familial nature of genetic testing and the possible needs of family members, this same Protocol also states that ‘exceptionally, and by derogation from the provisions of…article 10…the law may allow a genetic test to be carried out, for the benefit of family members, on a person who does not have the capacity to consent, if some specific conditions are met…[they include tests]…to allow the family member(s) concerned to obtain a preventive, diagnostic or therapeutic benefit that has been independently evaluated as important for their health…[and if] the expected benefit has been independently evaluated as substantially outweighing the risk for private life that may arise from the collection, processing or communication of the results of the test'' (art. 13). This position recognizes the notion of familial interests as a consideration in determining the child''s best interests. More recently, the European Society of Human Genetics (ESHG) published its Principles for Good Practice in Paediatric Biobanks , 12 which provided guidance on the return of results to minors in the specific context of biobanks. The Principles stated that ‘[t]he right of parents to receive or not receive genetic information about their children is limited.'' 4 , 5 But, ‘[i]n the rare case that information about a preventable or treatable early-onset disease is found, [parents] should be notified regardless of their wishes provided the findings are subject to assessment of clinical validity and utility.'' 6 At the national level, the 2010 British Society for Human Genetics Report on the Testing of Children , making no distinction between the research and clinical contexts, recommended against both predictive and presymptomatic genetic testing. ‘[I]n such circumstances testing should normally be delayed until the young person can decide for him/herself when, or whether to be tested.'' 13 Similarly, the 2012 Canadian Best Practices for Research Involving Children and Adolescents: Genetic, Pharmaceutical, and Longitudinal Studies 14 maintained that ‘individual research results and incidental findings should be communicated (1) if they are scientifically valid, (2) if they have significant implications for the health of the child or adolescent, and (3) if a treatment or method of prevention is available during childhood or adolescence'' (chapter 8). Emerging guidance In 2013, both the American Academy of Pediatrics and the American College of Medical Genetics and Genomics provided both a Policy Statement on the Ethical and Policy Issues in Genetic Testing and Screening of Children 4 , 5 and a Technical Report: Ethical and Policy Issues in Genetic Testing and Screening of Children . 5 This Policy Statement , as well as the Technical Report , reaffirmed that decisions about offering genetic testing and screening should be driven by the best interests of the child. It went on to support the traditional professional recommendations to defer genetic testing for late-onset conditions until adulthood. Yet, the Technical Report also stipulated that ‘predictive genetic testing may be appropriate in limited circumstances…. In deciding whether a child should undergo predictive genetic testing for late-onset conditions, the focus must be on the child''s medical best interests; however, parents and guardians may also consider the potential psychosocial benefits and harms to the child and the extended family.'' Thus, it conceded ‘[e]xtending consideration beyond the child''s medical best interest…recogniz[ing] that the interest of a child is embedded in and dependent on the interests of the family unit.'' It concluded that ‘[a]fter careful genetic counselling, it may be ethically acceptable to proceed with predictive genetic testing to resolve disabling parental anxiety or to support life-planning decisions that parents sincerely believe to be in the child''s best interest.'' This recognition of the possible legitimacy of familial and reproductive interests thus broadened the narrow ‘paediatric-actionability'' criterion. Not long thereafter, however, the American College of Medical Genetics and Genomics adopted a more radical and controversial position. It mandated obligatory testing of a panel of 57 reportable genetic conditions by laboratories undertaking WGS irrespective of whether adults or children are involved or of any personal parental choice other than to not have the indicated test at all. 15 , 16 Additionally, some authors have also suggested that paediatric researchers have a ‘limited responsibility'' to disclose certain genomic research findings. 17 This includes findings ‘of genetic variants with known, urgent clinical significance'' for the child. The criteria for what constitutes ‘urgent clinical significance'' include clear and direct benefit for the child and that the benefit outweighs the potential risks for psychological and social harm. Other considerations include the fact that ‘[r]esearchers already have been able to help clinicians aid some children born with rare birth defects by sequencing and analysing their whole genomes to diagnose and treat their illnesses.'' Such ‘medical'' sequencing, while justified for treatment purposes, still raises concerns ‘with regard to fully informed decision making'' since ‘whole-genome sequence data obtained from a minor already could have been widely shared before the minor reached an age at which they could determine preferred data sharing limits themselves, thereby decreasing their autonomy.'' 18 Further, the emergence of paediatric longitudinal biobank studies further complicates the WGS issues surrounding the return of results as asymptomatic children may be enrolled at birth. 19 Approaches from adult testing As WGS enters both paediatric research and even clinical practice for children with rare disorders, some lessons can also be drawn from the use of WGS in research involving adults. A number of approaches have sought to limit the amount of unsolicited information revealed. First, filters can restrict testing by the laboratory to only the research results actually sought. Second, binning regroups results into categories based on scientific validity, clinical utility, and actionability. A third approach uses an independent, multidisciplinary clinical oversight committee to act in an advisory capacity. However, all three are complicated in paediatric research by the exercise of parental authority and choice over health decisions concerning their children. 20 Relevant laws Finally, although not specific to genetic information, a recent overview of laws and policies underscored the lack of guidelines specific to the paediatric research context. 21 The authors identified a number of issues that need to be included: more transparency on the return of general research results; the need to distinguish between therapeutic (actionable) results and those that are of unknown clinical significance; the need to consider the specific condition and context of the study; and the mode, timing and delivery of validated research results. To this, we could add the need to inform parents of any policy concerning future recontact (or not), as well as the possibility of communicating a change of interpretation of any results or findings during childhood. It is against this international background on research and genetic testing involving children that the recommendations of the P3G Paediatric Platform on the communication of WGS should be situated. Recommendations We propose the following recommendations for sharing WGS results as being in the best interests of the child: The issue of the possible return (or not) of WGS results should be discussed during the informed consent process. During the consent/assent process, the child''s or adolescent''s views should be solicited and given due weight and consideration in accordance with his or her age and maturity. WGS results that are scientifically valid, clinically useful, and reveal conditions that are preventable and actionable during childhood should be offered. Mutations that predispose the child to develop an adult-onset disorder, even if accidentally discovered in the research process, generally should not be returned. This allows the child to make his or her own decision about receiving the results as an adult. Questions, which should arise rarely, of whether the child would benefit, on balance, from disclosure because of the potential benefit to the family from knowing about a highly penetrant gene they may have that poses serious risk to health and that is preventable or treatable, should be assessed on a case-by-case basis. This is a cautious approach and WGS may well become routine one day, perhaps even at birth. We have avoided defining scientific terms such as ‘validity'', ‘utility'' or ‘prevention'' believing them to be the purview of professional societies and scientific analysis. The aim is to not only provide a flexible approach for practice but also to recognize the need for clinical judgment 22 as concerns both WGS and future advances. When countries begin to introduce WGS as a routine paediatric diagnostic tool for more precise and earlier diagnosis and treatment, both the duties of researchers and the standard of care will be clarified. Till then, vulnerable children should not be caught up in the current web of uncertainty surrounding the return of WGS results. Acknowledgments This work was supported by grants from the Canadian Institutes of Health Research and the Terry Fox Foundation (TFF-105266), Genome Canada and the Canadian Institutes of Health Research, Finding of Rare Disease Genes in Canada (FORGE), the Maternal Infant and Youth Research Network (MICYRN), the Network of Applied Genetic Medicine of Québec (RMGA) and the Fonds de recherche en santé du Québec (FRSQ). APPENDIX Annelien L Bredenoord 1 , Alison Hall 2 , Kristien Hens 3 , Wim Pinxten 4 , Susan Wallace 5 , David Parry 6 , Ellen Wright Clayton 7 1 Division Julius Center, Medical Humanities, UMC Utrecht; 2 PHG Foundation, Cambridge, UK; 3 Department of Health, Ethics and Society, Maastricht University; 4 Department of Medical Ethics and Philosophy of Medicine, Erasmus, MC; 5 Department of Health Sciences, University of Leicester; 6 Centre of Genomics and Policy, McGill University; 7 Vanderbilt University Law School. The authors declare no conflict of interest. Contributor Information Ma''n H Zawati along with the P3G International Paediatrics Platform members: Annelien L Bredenoord , Alison Hall , Kristien Hens , Wim Pinxten , Susan Wallace , David Parry , and Ellen Wright Clayton References Saunders CJ, Miller NA, Soden SE, et al. Rapid whole-genome sequencing for genetic disease diagnosis in neonatal intensive care units. Science Translation Medicine. 2012;4:154ra135. doi: 10.1126/scitranslmed.3004041. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] United Nations . International Convention on the Rights of the Child, article 3. New York: United Nations; 1989. [ Google Scholar ] United Nations . International Convention on the Rights of the Child, article 12. New York: United Nations; 1989. [ Google Scholar ] American Academy of Pediatrics and American College of Medical Genetics and Genomics Policy Statement: Ethical and Policy Issues in Genetic Testing and Screening of Children. Pediatrics. 2013;131:620–622. doi: 10.1542/peds.2012-3680. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] Ross LF, Saal HM, David KL, et al. Technical Report: Ethical and Policy Issues in Genetic Testing and Screening of Children. Gene Med. 2013;15:234–245. doi: 10.1038/gim.2012.176. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] Knoppers BM. Paediatric research and the communication of not-so incidental findings. Paediatr Child Health J. 2012;17:190–192. doi: 10.1093/pch/17.4.190. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] PHG Foundation . Next Steps in the Sequence: The Implications of Whole Genome Sequencing for Health in the UK. Cambridge: PHG Foundation; 2011. [ Google Scholar ] Bredenoord AL, De Vries MC, Van Delden JJM. Next generation sequencing: does the next generation still have a right to an open future. Nat Rev Genet. 2013;14:306. doi: 10.1038/nrg3459. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] Noor AA, Giesbertz NAA, Bredenoord AL, van Delden JJM.Clarifying assent in pediatric research Eur J Hum Genet 2013;. e-pub ahead of print 12 June 2013; doi: 10.1038/ejhg.2013.119 [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] Council of Europe . Additional Protocol to the Convention on Human Rights and Biomedicine, concerning Biomedical Research. Strasbourg: Council of Europe; 2005. [ Google Scholar ] Council of Europe . Additional Protocol to the Convention on Human Rights and Biomedicine, concerning Genetic Testing for Health Purposes. Strasbourg: Council of Europe; 2008. [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] Hens K, Van EI CE, Borry P, et al. Developing a policy for paediatric biobanks: principles for good practice. Eur J Hum Genet. 2013;21:2–7. doi: 10.1038/ejhg.2012.99. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] British Society of Human Genetics . Report on the Testing of Children. Birmingham: British Society of Human Genetics; 2010. [ Google Scholar ] Centre of Genomics and Policy (CGP), Maternal Infant Child and Youth Research Network (MICYRN) Best Practices for Health Research Involving Children and Adolescents. Montreal; 2012. [ Google Scholar ] American College of Medical Genetics and Genomics . ACMG Recommendations for Reporting of Incidental Findings in Clinical Exome and Genome Sequencing. Bethesda: American College of Medical Genetics and Genomics; 2013. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] Burke W, Antommaria A, Bennett R, et al. Recommendations for returning genomic incidental findings? we need to talk! Genet MedForthcoming2013;. doi: 10.1038/gim.2013.113 [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] Abdul-Karim R. Disclosure of incidental findings from next-generation sequencing in pediatric genomic research. Pediatrics. 2013;131:564–571. doi: 10.1542/peds.2012-0084. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] United States Presidential Commission for the Study of Bioethical Issues . Privacy and Progress in Whole Genome Sequencing. Washington; 2012. [ Google Scholar ] Anastova V, Mahalatchimy A, Rial-Sebbag E, et al. Communication of results and disclosure of incidental findings in longitudinal paediatric research. Pediatr Allergy Immunol. 2013;24:389–394. doi: 10.1111/pai.12087. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] Knoppers BM, Rioux A, Zawati MH. Pediatric research ‘personalized''?: international perspectives on the return of results. Personalized Med. 2013;10:89–95. doi: 10.2217/pme.12.110. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] Avard D, Sénécal K, Madadi P, Sinnett D. Pediatric research and the return of individual research results. J Law Med Ethics. 2011;39:593–605. doi: 10.1111/j.1748-720X.2011.00626.x. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] McGuire AL, McCullough LB, Evans JP. The indispensable role of professional judgment in genomic medicine. JAMA. 2013;309:1465–1466. doi: 10.1001/jama.2013.1438. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ]',301,'2025-10-10 07:54:49','2025-10-10 07:54:49');
INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(1278,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3805618','Development and validation of a self-efficacy scale for clinical decision-making in general paediatrics.','Dandavino M, Young M, Gosselin R, Snell L, Bhanji F.','2013','gpedsse gpedsse ngse gpedsse ngse gpedsse gpedsse ngse gpedsse ngse gpedsse contextualizes historique reussite tache seulement tributaire efficacite confiance capacite executer tache mesurer efficacite prises elaborer fournir preuves validite specifique gpedsse efficacite methodologie chercheurs jumele gpedsse cinq elaboree nouvelle ngse efficacite analyser validite trente rempli gpedsse ngse entremeles resultat gpedsse totaux echelles gpedsse ngse presentaient moderee comparaison planifiee resultat gpedsse augmentait ngse exploratoires revele chacun harmonisant gpedsse existant represente concomitante novatrice validite henin gpedsse gpedsse rereading ngse ngse gpedsse ngse ngse ngse gpedsse ngse gpedsse gpedsse ngse gpedsse ngse gpedsse ngse gpedsse ngse varimax fluctuate gpedsse ngse gpedsse ngse gpedsse ngse gpedsse pasw ngse gpedsse gpedsse ngse ngse gpedsse gpedsse ngse gpedsse ngse ngse tough gpedsse gpedsse ngse unmodified gpedsse ngse gpedsse ngse gpedsse ngse ngse gpedsse gpedsse ngse gpedsse gpedsse ngse gpedsse ngse gpedsse gpedsse ngse tough ngse gpedsse gpedsse ngse fluctuate henin gpedsse contextualization gpedsse switalski s38 s43 1578j cull yudkowsky shipman gaulthier issenman pch takayama hilliard e131 sectish zalneraitis behrman macnab wambera 0b013e31803080c8 kolarik schwarzer weinman windsor nfer blok kerckhoffs kootstra edhep dierselhuis draaisma jmt otj ellershaw expectancies feigenbaum pasternak vinker gully sherer maddux mercandante coryell barbaranelli caprara pastorelli shapers scherbaum charash reformulation s46 dory pestiaux althouse stockman','Abstract BACKGROUND: Success in a task is not only related to skill, but it is also related to ‘self-efficacy’, or belief in one’s capability to perform that task. No tool currently exists to measure self-efficacy in clinical decision-making in general paediatrics. OBJECTIVE: To develop and provide validity evidence for the General Pediatrics-specific Self-Efficacy (GPedsSE) scale. METHODS: The five-item GPedsSE scale, developed using an expert panel, was matched to the New General Self-Efficacy (NGSE) scale’s structure for validity analysis purposes. Thirty-six postgraduate year 1 to postgraduate year 5 paediatric residents of the Montreal Children’s Hospital (Montreal, Quebec) completed the GPedsSE and NGSE scales, with items interspersed. RESULTS: The mean (± SD) GPedsSE score was 18.6±2.6 of 25. Total GPedsSE and NGSE scores were moderately correlated (r=0.54, P<0.005). On planned comparison, the GPedsSE score increased with training year (F[1.3]=6.62; P<0.001), while the NGSE score did not (F<0.37). Exploratory factor analysis showed two components, each aligning with a scale. CONCLUSION: The GPedsSE scale contextualizes an existing tool to general paediatrics, a novel concurrent validity approach. Keywords: General paediatrics, Medical, Residency, Self-efficacy Abstract HISTORIQUE : La réussite d’une tâche n’est pas seulement tributaire d’une compétence, mais également de « l’auto-efficacité » ou de la confiance en sa capacité d’exécuter cette tâche. Il n’existe pas d’outil pour mesurer l’auto-efficacité dans le cadre des prises de décision cliniques en pédiatrie générale. OBJECTIF : Élaborer et fournir des preuves de validité de l’échelle spécifique GPedsSE d’auto-efficacité en pédiatrie générale. MÉTHODOLOGIE : Les chercheurs ont jumelé l’échelle GPedsSE en cinq points, élaborée par un groupe d’experts, avec la structure de la nouvelle échelle NGSE d’auto-efficacité générale afin d’en analyser la validité. Trente-six résidents de 1 re à 5 e année de pédiatrie à L’Hôpital de Montréal pour enfants, au Québec, ont rempli l’échelle GPedsSE et l’échelle NGSE, dont les points étaient entremêlés. RÉSULTATS : Le résultat moyen (±ÉT) de l’échelle GPedsSE s’est élevé à 18,6±2,6 sur 25. Les résultats totaux des échelles GPedsSE et NGSE présentaient une corrélation modérée (r=0,54, P<0,005). Lors de la comparaison planifiée, le résultat de l’échelle GPedsSE augmentait selon l’année de formation (F[1,3]=6,62; P<0,001), mais pas celui de l’échelle NGSE (F<0,37). L’analyse des facteurs exploratoires a révélé deux éléments, chacun s’harmonisant à une échelle. CONCLUSION : L’échelle GPedsSE met un outil existant dans le contexte de la pédiatrie, ce qui représente une approche concomitante novatrice de la validité. Paediatric primary care in Canada and elsewhere is changing. General paediatricians are now caring for an increasing number of children with complex medical conditions ( 1 , 2 ) and are acting as consultants to other providers of paediatric care ( 3 – 5 ). Although solid knowledge and skills in the area of care to the medically complex child is warranted, competence in the management of basic and common general paediatrics problems encountered in both the hospital-based and the ambulatory care setting remains essential to provide effective consultation expertise. Developing clinical decision-making skills in general paediatrics is challenging for residents enrolled in tertiary centre-based paediatric residency programs. Recent surveys of graduates found that they were comfortable managing acutely sick children in the inpatient setting ( 6 – 9 ), but believed themselves to be ill-prepared to deal with several common ambulatory general paediatrics problems ( 7 , 8 , 10 , 11 ) (unpublished data, Henin C, 2006). The belief in one’s ability to make appropriate management decisions when faced with problematic situations is known as ‘self-efficacy’ ( 12 – 14 ). The low self-efficacy of newly graduated paediatricians in managing general paediatrics problems is concerning because low self-efficacy is linked to the decreased performance of tasks ( 12 – 16 ). Low self-efficacy toward a task could also negatively impact its chances of success ( 12 ) by decreasing how much effort is invested, persistence in the face of adversity and assurance when approaching that task ( 13 ). While previous studies have explored constructs of self-efficacy in specific contexts, roles or tasks within the realm of clinical medicine ( 10 , 16 , 17 ), none have focused on clinical decision-making skills in general, and none have investigated the domain of general paediatrics. The goal of the present study was to develop and provide evidence for the validity of a General Pediatrics-specific Self-Efficacy (GPedsSE) scale to evaluate self-efficacy specific to clinical decision-making in general paediatrics. If successfully validated, this tool could be one of the measures used to monitor the development of residents’ clinical decision-making in general paediatrics. METHODS Tool development To the authors’ knowledge, there are no published reports characterizing ‘confidence’ in clinical decision-making in general paediatrics. Due to the absence of literature on which to base the GPedsSE item generation, expert opinion was sought. This approach has been used previously in related studies to determine or validate the content of task-specific self-efficacy tools ( 15 , 17 , 18 ). The principal author conducted semistructured, face-to-face individual interviews with a purposive sample consisting of three general paediatricians with mixed hospital and community/ambulatory practices, who had been in practice for four, 16 and 35 years, respectively. After explaining the concept of self-efficacy, these experts were asked about their perception of what the characteristics of ‘confident’ clinical decision-making or self-efficacy in general paediatrics may be. Written notes were taken, and an inductive process of reading and rereading allowed for content analysis and the generation of five key themes ( Table 1 ). Because of apparent saturation of themes, additional experts were not sought. TABLE 1. Expert-identified characteristics of ‘confident’ decision-making in general paediatrics Characteristics Confident in the assessment of problem severity Knows when there is a need for further investigations Knows when there is a need for referral to a subspecialist Confident in the management decisions regarding common problems Confident in management decisions for difficult cases Open in a new tab Although originally domain-specific ( 12 ), expansion of Bandura’s concept of self-efficacy to reflect one’s overall confidence in their ability to manage demanding or novel situations ( 14 ) allowed for the development of so-called ‘general self-efficacy’ questionnaires, such as the New General Self-Efficacy (NGSE) scale ( 14 , 19 , 20 ). The NGSE scale is an eight-item, self-administered questionnaire that requires the user to indicate agreement with a series of statements (eg, “I believe I can succeed at most any endeavor to which I set my mind”) on a five-point Likert scale with anchors ranging from ‘Strongly disagree’ to ‘Strongly agree’, for a maximum score of 40. It was decided to conduct validity analyses through comparison of the GPedsSE with the NGSE scale; therefore, it was necessary that the newly-generated general paediatrics items be as comparable as possible with the NGSE scale’s items. The construction of the items were matched, including the response categories and the wording of response with those of the NGSE scale. This also facilitated item presentation, because items from the GPedsSE and NGSE scales could be randomly interspersed when administered to participants. Because the GPedsSE scale was designed to be short and easy to use, one item for each of the five expert-generated themes was created ( Table 1 ). Responses were based on a five-point Likert scale using anchors ranging from ‘Strongly disagree’ to ‘Strongly agree’, for a maximum score of 25. Setting and subjects All residents training in the core paediatric residency program (postgraduate year [PGY] 1 to 3) during the 2009/2010 academic year at the Montreal Children’s Hospital (Montreal, Quebec), an academic tertiary care paediatric hospital affiliated with McGill University (Montreal, Quebec), were invited to participate. A convenience sample of recent graduates (PGY5), who were pursuing further paediatric specialty training after successfully completing their paediatrics certifying examination, were also invited to participate. No PGY4 residents were training in the program during that academic year. All invited residents agreed, provided written informed consent and completed a self-administered questionnaire that included the GPedsSE and NGSE items. All questionnaires were anonymized, and identifying information replaced by numerical codes before analysis. The present study was approved by the Research Ethics Board of the Faculty of Medicine, McGill University. Rationale and statistical analyses for tool validation Concurrent validity: Rationale: While self-efficacy for clinical decision-making in general paediatrics presumably is a unique and specific construct, it is likely to be at least conceptually related to an individual’s general self-efficacy when dealing with stressful situations (ie, ‘general self-efficacy’). That is, a paediatric resident’s self-efficacy in his/her ability to manage stressful situations in general may translate into a higher self-efficacy when faced with general paediatrics clinical challenges. Hence, in the present study, it was believed that concurrent validity would be supported by a moderate, but not strong, relationship between scores on the GPedsSE and the NGSE scales ( 5 , 21 ). A strong correlation would suggest both scales are measuring the same, rather than related, constructs. Statistical analysis: The total scores on the GPedsSE and NGSE scales were correlated using Pearson correlation coefficients. A factor analysis was conducted using all items (both GPedsSE and NGSE) using the principal component method, and data were rotated using a varimax rotation and Kaiser normalization to examine the underlying structure of the questionnaire. Construct validity: Rationale: The progression through core paediatric training should positively influence general paediatrics-specific self-efficacy, as the resident develops a more complete paediatric knowledge base and skillset, and is assuming an increasing level of responsibility in paediatric care. On the other hand, general self-efficacy – a stable personality trait believed to fluctuate over long time periods in an individual’s lifetime ( 20 ) – is not predicted to change over a four-year time span. For the present study, it was postulated that construct validity would be evidenced through finding increasing GPedsSE scores across training level and no such increase in NGSE scores. Statistical analysis: Total scores on the GPedsSE and NGSE scales across training years (PGY1 to PGY5) using a planned comparisons ANOVA, with training year as a between-subjects factor and total score on the GPedsSE and NGSE scales as the variable of interest, were analyzed. Exploration of between-subjects effects: Rationale: Self-efficacy is known to vary according to sex ( 22 ), and could differ for trainees from outside of Canada, because some trainees from outside of Canada enter residency with previous paediatric training experiences. Statistical analysis: The effects of sex (male versus female) and of place of undergraduate training (Canada versus all others) on the GPedsSE score using independent samples t tests were analyzed. Other analyses: In addition to the above mentioned analyses, descriptive analyses of scale responses calculating means ± SD were conducted; P<0.05 was considered to be statistically significant. All analyses were performed using PASW Statistics 18 (IBM Corporation, USA). RESULTS Sample characteristics Participants’ characteristics can be found in Table 2 . TABLE 2. Respondents’ demographics (n=36) Demographic variable Sex Male 12 Female 24 Location of undergraduate medical training Canada 22 Other 14 Training year 1 11 2 10 3 10 5 5 Open in a new tab Data presented as n Descriptive results The mean NGSE (± SD) score was 31.48±3.32 of 40, which is comparable to mean scores previously reported for the general population ( 23 ). The mean GPedsSE score was 18.64±2.6 of 25. Concurrent validity Overall, total GPedsSE score correlated moderately, but significantly, with total NGSE score (r[29]=0.54; P<0.005). Factor analysis revealed two primary components explaining 58.7% of the variance, with component 1 generally aligning with the NGSE scale and component 2 with the GPedsSE scale ( Table 3 ). Two of the five questions from the GPedsSE scale did not fully cluster independently of the NGSE questions ( Table 3 ). TABLE 3. Results of exploratory factor analysis investigating the internal structure of the General Pediatrics-specific Self-Efficacy (GPedsSE) and New General Self-Efficacy (NGSE) scales Scale Scale item Component 1 2 NGSE I will be able to achieve most of the goals that I have set for myself 0.581 When facing difficult tasks, I am certain that I will accomplish them 0.770 In general, I think that I can obtain outcomes that are important to me 0.812 I believe I can succeed at most any endeavor to which I set my mind 0.813 I will be able to successfully overcome many challenges 0.849 I am confident that I can perform effectively on many different tasks 0.771 * Compared to other people, I can do most tasks very well 0.694 0.477 Even when things are tough, I can perform quite well 0.440 GPedsSE * I am confident that I know when to run investigations for general paediatrics problems 0.570 0.503 I will know when it is time to refer a general paediatrics problem to a subspecialist 0.618 In a general paediatrics context, I am confident that I would not miss severe disease 0.915 * I believe that I can manage common general paediatrics problems 0.559 0.434 In a general paediatrics context, when facing a difficult case, I am certain I can make the right management decisions 0.792 Open in a new tab * Items that did not cluster fully independently Construct validity On planned comparison ANOVA, the GPedsSE score increased with training year (F[3,32]=6.62; P<0.001), while the NGSE score did not (F[3,32]=0.37; P=0.77) ( Figure 1 ). Although unmodified total scores were used in the analysis, scores were converted to percentage of maximum to allow for comparability across the two scales and for clarity of presentation in Figure 1 . Table 4 shows the score distribution within each training year for each scale. Figure 1). Open in a new tab Paediatric residents’ scores for the General Pediatrics-specific Self-Efficacy (GPedsSE) and the New General Self-Efficacy (NGSE) scales and postgraduate year. Total scores were converted to per cent of maximum score for the purposes of clarity and comparability TABLE 4. Paediatric residents’ scores for the General Pediatrics-specific Self-Efficacy (GPedsSE) and the New General Self-Efficacy (NGSE) scales PGY n Mean ± SD Range GPedsSE 1 11 18.00±2.68 15–23 2 10 18.30±1.49 16–21 3 10 19.70±2.31 17–25 5 5 22.80±1.30 21–24 NGSE 1 11 31.91±4.32 27–40 2 10 31.20±2.49 28–37 3 10 31.30±3.71 25–37 5 5 33.00±1.41 31–34 Open in a new tab PGY Postgraduate year Exploration of between-subjects effects There was no significant difference between male and female residents on either the NGSE (males 31.9±4.3, females 31.6±2.84; t [29]=0.78; P=0.44) or the GPedsSE scales (males 18.4±1.93, females 19.6±2.84; t [29]=0.19; P=0.85). There was a significant difference between location of undergraduate training and GPedsSE scores, with residents trained outside of Canada scoring significantly higher than residents trained in Canada (residents trained outside of Canada 19.19±2.54, residents trained in Canada 17.71±2.64; t [29]=2.79; P<0.01) while no difference was found for the NGSE score (residents trained outside of Canada 32.43±4.35, residents trained in Canada 30.72±2.49; t [29]=1.38; P=0.18). DISCUSSION In the present study, we developed and provided some evidence of validity for a tool (the GPedsSE scale) designed to measure self-efficacy for clinical decision-making in general paediatrics. The validation of our scale was based on the hypothesis that general paediatrics-specific clinical decision-making self-efficacy and general self-efficacy are different but related constructs. While the total scores on both the GPedsSE and NGSE scales moderately correlated, exploratory factor analysis demonstrated two discrete components, each generally aligning with a scale. The GPedsSE and NGSE scales were thus measuring somewhat different, but related, constructs. Two GPedsSE items overlapped both components identified in the factor analysis (“I am confident that I know when to run investigations for general paediatrics problems” and “I believe that I can manage common general paediatrics problems”). These items may be more general in nature than the other three GPedsSE items, and their overlap with both components may be the result of a less discriminatory performance. We believe that, even though overlap occurred, the items generated by our expert panel represent general paediatrics-specific self-efficacy, and so opted to keep this item in our scale. Decreased accuracy is a known issue when general questions are used in self-assessment ( 24 ). This phenomenon may also explain why the very general NGSE item “Even when things are tough, I can perform quite well” did not load onto the NGSE component and weakly loaded onto the GPedsSE component. The GPedsSE scores increased with advancing level of training. Theoretically, self-efficacy in general paediatrics clinical decision-making may have increased due to personal successes in management of cases during residency, observation of resident peers performing successfully and positive feedback from tutors, in addition to an internal confidence of increasing knowledge and skills ( 13 , 25 ). The NGSE scores, on the other hand, showed no such increase across training levels. Although general self-efficacy may change throughout the lifespan of an individual ( 26 ), it is believed to fluctuate only over long periods of time ( 20 ). While previous studies reported low self-efficacy toward common ambulatory general paediatrics problems in newly graduated paediatricians ( 1 , 5 , 7 , 9 ) (unpublished data, Henin C, 2006) our study suggests that it does increase across training. The GPedsSE tool could be used to monitor the self-efficacy in decision-making in trainees of general paediatrics, or could perhaps assist in the detection of performance outliers. Guided by the theoretical framework of how self-efficacy impacts practice ( 10 , 12 ), residency programs could introduce measures to enhance their residents’ self-efficacy ( 12 , 13 , 22 ) and potentially positively impact their clinical decision-making skills in general paediatrics. The importance of addressing self-efficacy issues in training curricula is increasingly recognized in medical education ( 16 ). We explored potential determinants of self-efficacy in general paediatrics, such as sex and location of undergraduate medical school training. We found no significant difference between the self-efficacy in clinical decision-making between male and female paediatric residents in our program. This contrasts with the usual report of higher self-efficacy among men in science-related tasks and within fields historically considered ‘nontraditional’ for women ( 22 ). Whether this can be generalized to other cohorts should be investigated; however, the fact that paediatrics is an increasingly ‘female-based’ field ( 3 , 27 ) may, in part, explain this finding. Interestingly, we also found that residents whose medical school training took place outside of Canada demonstrated a higher self-efficacy toward general paediatrics problems than their Canadian-trained counterparts. This may be due, in part, to the fact that the majority of the non-Canadian trained residents have undergone one or more years of paediatric training before coming to Canada. The number of participants in our study did not allow investigation into whether non-Canadian trained residents began their training with higher general paediatric self-efficacy than the Canadian trainees, and/or whether their self-efficacy developed differently. There are some limitations to our study. First, our study was conducted at a single institution, with the number of participants limited by the size of our training program. Due to the size of our sample, the within-training year score variation may not be representative of other paediatric resident groups. Replication of the study using tracking of individual residents and using a larger population, for example including residents from other training sites, may lend further support to our study’s conclusions and may aid in determining how this scale could be used as a longitudinal tracking tool. Second, we developed our scale items using expert opinion due to the paucity of available data and lack of similar tools, and our items, therefore, may not represent a complete catalogue of self-efficacy measures for general paediatrics residents. Further research is also required to determine whether this tool can be used with other types of trainees who provide paediatric primary care (eg, family medicine residents). The ability of this tool to evaluate educational interventions targeting the enhancement of clinical decision-making and related self-efficacy in general paediatrics should also be investigated. Finally, additional research could explore how self-efficacy in clinical decision-making in general paediatrics transfers to performance on work-based clinical assessments, and to actual practice. CONCLUSION We developed a short, easy-to-administer, valid scale to assess the self-efficacy of paediatric residents in general paediatrics clinical decision-making. We developed this scale by explicitly integrating expert-based, context-specific items into a previously validated scale, a relatively novel method of scale contextualization to approach concurrent validity. Using the GPedsSE scale, we found an increase in self-efficacy in clinical decision-making in general paediatrics with advancing level of training in our paediatric residents. Other paediatric residency programs may wish to implement this tool to assess the general paediatrics self-efficacy of their trainees. This may assist in further validation of this tool and, eventually, justify its use as a source to inform curricula, track individual performances and guide remediation programs. REFERENCES 1. Freed GL, Dunham KM, Switalski KE, Jones MD, McGuinness GA, the Research Advisory Committee of the American Board of Pediatrics Recently trained general pediatricians: Perspectives on residency training and scope of practice. Pediatrics. 2009;123(Supplement):S38–S43. doi: 10.1542/peds.2008-1578J. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 2. Hewitt-Taylor J. Caring for children with complex needs: Staff education and training. J Child Health Care. 2005;9:72–86. doi: 10.1177/1367493505050506. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 3. Cull WL, Yudkowsky BK, Shipman SA, Pan RJ, American Academy of Pediatrics Pediatric training and job market trends: Results from the American Academy of Pediatrics third-year resident survey, 1997–2002. Pediatrics. 2003;112:787–92. doi: 10.1542/peds.112.4.787. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 4. Gaulthier M, Issenman RM, Wilson I, Canadian Paediatric Society, Paediatric Human Resources Planning Committee A model of paediatrics: Rethinking health care for children and youth. Paediatr Child Health. 2009;14:319–32. doi: 10.1093/pch/14.5.319. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 5. Clark LA, Watson D. Constructing validity: Basic issues in objective scale development. Psychol Assess. 1995;7:3