 PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 40. Krekels EHJ, DeJongh J, van Lingen RA, et al. Predictive performance of a recently developed population pharmacokinetic model for morphine and its metabolites in new datasets of (preterm) neonates, infants and children. Clin Pharmacokinet 2011;50:51–63. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] Associated Data This section collects any data citations, data availability statements, or supplementary materials included in this article. Supplementary Materials Web supplement archdischild-2014-306853-s1.pdf (102.2KB, pdf)',28,'2025-10-10 07:55:36','2025-10-10 07:55:36');
INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(1282,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12306233','New generation of paediatricians in the health care system: are paediatric residents ready to manage children without supervision? A survey using respiratory infections as a model.','Del Bene M, Diana A, Corsello A, Cecere F, Indolfi C, Mozzillo E, Nunziata F, Pascarella F, Rossi F, Sibilio M, Ummarino M, Guarino A.','2025','lrtis lrtis lrti lrtis hws retirements retiring gimbe interregional fledged nearing postpandemic respivirnet lrtis stints lrtis lrtis lrtis replicable readministered hws lrtis onsp lrti hailed admittances lrti lrti lrti colds lrtis wielenga binotti customisation wealthier deepest pnrr onsp prepublication enquiry paterlini gimbe comunicato stampa maggio gimbe comunicato sisac convenzionata gennaio sisac actionpagina_296 bando accesso scuole specializzazione attiministeriali miur anno maggio ministeriale mur atti normativa ministeriale enpam enpam libera norma tampone convenzionati ghirardo ullmann zago antonino emanuela sorveglianza integrata respiratori respivirnet stagione boll naz epicentro ben_98 ben_98 langhan emer e1462 minh duc luu thang 320_pedsmilestonesproject wielenga ega taris wigboldus dhj deci ceps kilminster navon naveh mnsc deci villaizan aparicio istat mitzman e40142 krupat brower wenrich cynicism binotti genoni rizzollo mittiga pappalardo fanelli chine children8040260','Abstract Abstract Objectives To measure the perceived autonomy level in managing lower respiratory tract infections (LRTIs) among paediatric residents and to compare perceived autonomy with the assessments by tutors who directly supervise and evaluate residents Design/setting A survey on managing LRTIs was distributed among all Italian paediatric residents and tutors via Google Forms between January 1 and 31, 2024. Participants, residents and tutors were classified according to their training year and clinical setting: primary care paediatrics (PCP), emergency department (ED) and hospital ward (HW). Outcome Perceived autonomy of paediatric residents in managing LRTI in different settings; overall perceived autonomy and interest in employment. Results A total of 391 questionnaires were returned, with 273 completed by residents and 118 by tutors. Among residents, 3% in their first year felt fully capable of managing LRTIs without supervision in both ED and HW settings. This percentage remained below 25% until the third year and increased to 72% in ED and 83% in HW by the fifth year. In PCP settings, autonomy ranged from 15% in the first year to 96% in the final year. No differences were found between residents’ self-evaluations and tutors’ assessments. Confidence in skills showed no regional differences across Italy. Conclusion Most residents did not feel ready to manage winter respiratory infections in children without supervision, especially in the ED compared with the HW; however, self-confidence was higher in the PCP setting. Tutors shared similar perceptions. The location did not affect the response pattern. These findings caution against employing residents to work autonomously, particularly in emergency settings. Keywords: Public health, PAEDIATRICS, MEDICAL EDUCATION & TRAINING STRENGTHS AND LIMITATIONS OF THIS STUDY. The questionnaire has been created by a multidisciplinary team dedicated to paediatric residents’ education. The survey was distributed directly through social media channels and therefore reached a large population of residents and tutors. We used two parallel surveys directed at paediatric residents and tutors in different settings (primary care paediatrics, emergency departments and hospital wards), focusing on various aspects of managing a child, including key medical decisions and clinical reasoning. Despite efforts to increase participation, one notable limitation of this study was the low response rate. Introduction The Italian healthcare system provides free healthcare to all children across three care levels. The primary care level involves approximately 8000 family paediatricians, each responsible for 1000–1500 children aged 0–14 years. High-level emergency care for children is provided by specialised emergency units in large regional hospitals. In smaller centres, emergency care is typically provided by general physicians with limited paediatric experience, though paediatricians are sometimes available. The third level of care is represented by specialised children’s hospital wards (HWs). Italy is experiencing a rapid decline in the number of physicians, driven by a significant increase in retirements. This trend is particularly pronounced in paediatric care, which is age-specific in all medical settings. The imbalance between retiring paediatricians and those entering the medical workforce stems from the widespread scarcity of medical professionals, a paramount concern for the National Health Service (NHS). 1 This scarcity is a consequence of previous cost containment measures and inadequate long-term planning, significantly impacting healthcare delivery in both hospital and community settings. According to the GIMBE 2023 report 2 and the Interregional Health Care Affiliated Structure, 3 each family paediatrician is tasked with an average of 200 additional children beyond the recommended limit of 800 patients. Due to this shortage, the number of paediatric residents has been progressively increased, with more than 3000 residents nationwide in 2023. The total number of training residents comprises approximately 20% of the total working paediatricians and continues to increase. Despite a significant rise in fellowships for paediatric residency programmes over the past 5 years—escalating from 440 in the 2016–2017 academic year 4 to 885 in 2023–2024 5 —the influx of new paediatricians is estimated to still fall short of meeting the demands of generational turnover. 6 However, during and after the COVID-19 emergency, residents from various disciplines were enrolled to work autonomously as full-fledged doctors, typically in hospital settings, while completing their final 2 years of residency programmes. With a workforce increasingly composed of physicians nearing retirement and young physicians yet to complete the 5-year course in paediatrics, the system faces additional challenges from the rebound rise in respiratory infections following the COVID-19 pandemic. A recent epidemiological study conducted in Italy demonstrated a nearly 70% increase in hospitalisations for bronchiolitis, accompanied by an increase in invasive respiratory support and coinfections. 7 These findings indicate a more severe disease trajectory postpandemic than in the previous 5 years. A peak in respiratory infections, including respiratory syncytial virus-induced bronchiolitis, influenza, adenovirus infections and COVID-19, was observed between late December 2023 and January 2024. Data from the RespiVirNet report 8 revealed that the average weekly incidence of respiratory infections in January 2024 was as high as 31.47 cases per 1000 in the 0–4-year group and 13.39 per 1000 in the 5–14-year-old age group. 8 The proliferation of respiratory viruses has inevitably resulted in a surge in emergency room admissions, exacerbating the burden on the medical workforce. Consequently, the management of lower respiratory tract infections (LRTIs) by paediatric residents can be seen as a stress test to explore options for healthcare challenges in paediatrics using a model of self-assessment of autonomy and competence. 9 In Italy, the national 5-year paediatric residency programme encompasses training stints in primary care paediatrics (PCP), emergency department (ED) and HW. This study aimed to evaluate the readiness of the NHS to address forthcoming epidemics of respiratory infections in children across these three distinct settings. The primary objective was to measure the perceived autonomy level in managing LRTIs among paediatric residents, whereas the secondary objective was to compare perceived autonomy with the assessments by tutors who directly supervise and evaluate residents. Tutors are usually assigned up to three training residents and typically evaluate their medical competence and operational autonomy. However, exceptions exist for some residents who were employed during the COVID-19 pandemic emergency as medical assistants, allowing them to function as hospital physicians without supervision. Political healthcare authorities are now considering extending employment to residents to face the shortage of specialists and to let them enter the NHS without preliminary formal evaluation of their training. Methods Design This cross-sectional study used a survey design to evaluate the perceived autonomy level in managing LRTIs among paediatric residents. This survey study is reported according to Checklist for Reporting Of Survey Studies. 10 Survey development and content A multidisciplinary team of university physicians, urgent care providers and family paediatricians developed two parallel surveys: a 47-item survey addressing paediatric residents and a 38-item survey targeting tutors. Each questionnaire included multiple choice questions rated on a 5-point Likert scale. Both surveys were divided into three subsets focused on personal information, the management of LRTIs according to the current rotation setting (PCP, ED and HW) and three overall autonomy levels and interest in signing a physician employment agreement for a job as an independent physician. The questionnaire addressed various aspects of managing a child, encompassing main medical decisions and clinical reasoning. The questions assessed self-confidence in a thorough clinical evaluation, diagnostic approach, indications for hospital admission, fluid and oxygen supplementation, therapy, monitoring and follow-up. For the development of the survey and to support the analysis plan, a conceptual framework was created through a systematic, replicable and iterative process. The first version of the questionnaire was administered to a sample of residents and tutors and readministered 15 days later to assess its reproducibility and validity. The key instrument targeting residents and tutors is provided in supplementary material (Survey S1 and S2). Patient and public involvement No patients were directly involved in this study. Instead, residents and paediatricians in tutor roles, working in PCP, EDs and HWs, were involved. They were asked questions regarding the clinical management of patients with LRTIs. 10 Survey distribution Both questionnaires were simultaneously distributed to all Italian paediatric residents (approximately 3000) and their tutors during the peak period of paediatric respiratory infections, from January 1 to 31, 2024. The survey was distributed directly by the National Observatory of Pediatric Residents (ONSP) through its official website page, newsletters and social media channels (ie, WhatsApp Messenger). Additionally, it was sent to the directors of all Italian paediatric residency programmes, who were tasked with distributing the survey to their tutors and residents. Respondents were instructed to complete the web-based survey only once. The survey did not include any clinical information about patients or physicians’ practices and solely explored practitioners’ behaviours and self-confidence levels. Participation in the survey was voluntary, anonymous and independent, with information confidentiality ensured and no financial incentives offered for participation. Responses were aggregated, and completion of the questionnaire implied participant consent. No formal ethical approval was required for this survey, and the study complied with the Declaration of Helsinki. Analysis plan We compared perceived autonomy in managing different aspects of LRTI between residents in different operational settings, further differentiating residents by year of residency, and directly compared their responses against the tutors’ evaluation. We also evaluated the overall perceived autonomy in managing a paediatric patient. As both the residents’ and the tutors’ questionnaires were anonymous, we could not directly compare the evaluation between a single resident and his tutor but compared the two groups as a whole. Statistical analysis Statistical analyses were performed using IBM SPSS Statistics for Windows, V.26.0 (Armonk, NY, USA: IBM Corp). Categorical variables, expressed as frequencies and percentages, were compared using Fisher’s exact or χ 2 tests. Statistical significance was set at p<0.05. Results Study population A total of 391 questionnaires were returned, 273 of which were completed by residents (accounting for almost 10% of total residents) and 118 by tutors. As many tutors do not directly affiliate with universities and the survey distribution was web-based, we cannot determine the percentage of tutors that replied to our survey. The residents and tutors hailed from various geographical areas. Residents and relative tutors were distributed from the first year (12% vs 11%) to the fifth year (17% vs 4%) of the residency programmes. Moreover, 30/273 (11%) residents already had employment agreements related to the COVID-19 emergency. Residents and tutors worked in different settings ( table 1 ). Table 1. Study population. Paediatric residents, n (%) Tutors, n (%) Italian area North 95 (35) 39 (33) Centre 80 (29) 33 (28) South 98 (36) 46 (39) Ongoing training period Emergency department 34 (13) 28 (23) Hospital ward 140 (52) 65 (54) Day hospital 24 (9) 14 (12) Medical clinic at hospital 51 (19) 3 (3) Medical clinic with family paediatrician 19 (7) 10 (8) Employment agreement 15 (5) Residents included in study population Year of residency programme First 33 (12) 13 (11) Second 52 (19) 33 (28) Third 64 (23) 45 (38) Fourth 77 (28) 22 (19) Fifth 47 (17) 5 (4) Open in a new tab We will express the majority of results indicating either significant data (ie, discrepancies between tutors’ evaluation and residents’ perception) or by expressing the number of residents (and their tutors’ evaluation) that felt independent in any given task. Lower respiratory tract infection management in different settings according to paediatric residents and their tutors A total of 52% of residents whose rotation was in the PCP reported the need for indirect supervision while handling telemedicine, including providing guidance related to direct visits, home monitoring of clinical parameters and recognising early warning signs, compared with 20% of tutors. Additionally, 52% felt the need for indirect supervision in evaluating patient history, clinical conditions and distress signs, whereas 10% of tutors believed they needed it. Only 20% of residents felt that they could assess the need for ED admission, compared with 40% of tutors ( table 2 ). Table 2. Evaluation of autonomy at primary care paediatrics. Paediatric residents’ self-evaluation, n (%) Paediatric residents’ tutor evaluation, n (%) P (<0.05) Autonomy in handling telemedicine 25 20 Completely independent 8 (32) 10 (50) 0.22 Need of indirect supervision 13 (52) 4 (20) 0.028 Need of direct supervision 4 (16) 6 (30) 0.26 Only observatory 0 (0) 0 (0) 1 χ 2 with 4×2 variables 0.09 Autonomy in collecting history and clinical evaluation 25 20 Completely independent 11 (44) 14 (70) 0.08 Need of indirect supervision 13 (52) 2 (10) 0.003 Need of direct supervision 1 (4) 4 (20) 0.09 Only observatory 0 (0) 0 (0) 1 χ 2 with 4×2 variables 0.007 Autonomy in evaluating the need for emergency department admission 25 20 Completely independent 5 (20) 8 (40) 0.14 Need of indirect supervision 13 (52) 6 (30) 0.14 Need of direct supervision 6 (24) 6 (30) 0.65 Only observatory 1 (4) 0 (0) 1 χ 2 with 4×2 variables 0.23 Open in a new tab Within the ED setting, 49% of residents believed that they could independently collect patient history and conduct clinical evaluations, including assessing the age-based respiratory rate (RR), peripheral saturation and chest auscultation and identifying respiratory distress signs, with 50% of tutors agreeing. Regarding specific tasks, 47% felt confident ordering laboratory tests and imaging (compared with 36% of tutors), 44% in initiating an intravenous programme (vs 36% of tutors) and 26% in prescribing therapy (compared with 32% of tutors). Notably, only 14% believed that they could autonomously manage oxygen therapy, with 7% of tutors agreeing. However, 63% of residents expressed a need for supervision when managing admissions and discharges, compared with 72% of tutors. Moreover, 66% required supervision when evaluating disease severity and determining the necessity for properly referring a child to a paediatric intensive care unit (PICU), compared with 79% of tutors ( table 3 ). Table 3. Evaluation of autonomy in the emergency department. Paediatric residents’ self-evaluation, n (%) Paediatric residents’ tutor evaluation, n (%) P (<0.05) Autonomy in collecting history and clinical evaluation 43 28 Completely independent 21 (49) 14 (50) 0.92 Need of indirect supervision 18 (42) 11 (39) 0.82 Need of direct supervision 3 (7) 3 (11) 0.58 Only observatory 1 (2) 0 (0) 1 χ 2 with 4×2 variables 0.94 Autonomy in laboratory and radiological test prescription 43 28 Completely independent 20 (47) 10 (36) 0.36 Need of indirect supervision 18 (42) 13 (46) 0.7 Need of direct supervision 4 (9) 4 (14) 0.5 Only observatory 1 (2) 1 (4) 0.75 χ 2 with 4×2 variables 0.8 Autonomy in therapy prescription 43 28 Completely independent 20 (26) 9 (32) 0.23 Need of indirect supervision 16 (37) 13 (46) 0.44 Need of direct supervision 4 (9) 4 (14) 0.5 Only observatory 3 (7) 2 (7) 0.98 χ 2 with 4×2 variables 0.66 Autonomy in electrolyte solution intravenous prescription 43 28 Completely independent 19 (44) 10 (36) 0.48 Need of indirect supervision 15 (35) 12 (43) 0.5 Need of direct supervision 7 (16) 4 (14) 0.79 Only observatory 2 (5) 2 (7) 0.66 χ 2 with 4×2 variables 0.85 Autonomy in oxygen therapy management 43 28 Completely independent 6 (14) 2 (7) 0.37 Need of indirect supervision 25 (58) 13 (46) 0.33 Need of direct supervision 10 (23) 10 (36) 0.25 Only observatory 2 (5) 3 (11) 0.33 χ 2 with 4×2 variables 0.4 Autonomy in admittances and discharge management 43 28 Completely independent 15 (35) 4 (14) 0.053 Need of indirect supervision 19 (44) 12 (43) 0.91 Need of direct supervision 8 (19) 8 (29) 0.33 Only observatory 1 (2) 4 (14) 0.054 χ 2 with 4×2 variables 0.07 Autonomy in disease severity evaluation and assessment of PICU requirements 43 28 Completely independent 11 (26) 3 (11) 0.12 Need of indirect supervision 20 (47) 10 (36) 0.37 Need of direct supervision 8 (19) 12 (43) 0.02 Only observatory 4 (9) 3 (11) 0.84 χ 2 with 4×2 variables 0.11 Open in a new tab PICU, paediatric intensive care unit. In HW, 48% of residents believed that they could collect history and conduct clinical evaluations without supervision, including assessing age-based RR, peripheral saturation and auscultation and identifying respiratory distress signs; however, only 24% of tutors agreed with this statement. Similarly, 35% felt confident in prescribing laboratory and radiology tests (with 27% of tutors in agreement), 35% in initiating an intravenous programme (with 20% of tutors agreeing), 22% in prescribing therapy (with 16% of tutors agreeing) and 24% in autonomously managing oxygen therapy (with 11% of tutors agreeing). Finally, 75% of residents expressed the need for supervision when managing discharges, compared with 81% of tutors, whereas 72% required supervision when evaluating disease severity and considering transfer to a PICU, compared with 76% of tutors ( table 4 ). Table 4. Evaluation of autonomy in the hospital ward. Paediatric residents’ self-evaluation, n (%) Paediatric residents’ tutor evaluation, n (%) P (<0.05) Autonomy in collecting history and clinical evaluation 120 63 Completely independent 57 (48) 15 (24) 0.001 Need of indirect supervision 54 (45) 30 (48) 0.73 Need of direct supervision 8 (7) 17 (27) 0.0001 Only observatory 1 (1) 1 (2) 0.64 χ 2 with 4×2 variables 0.0003 Autonomy in laboratory and radiological test prescription 120 63 Completely independent 42 (35) 17 (27) 0.27 Need of indirect supervision 66 (55) 24 (38) 0.03 Need of direct supervision 8 (7) 20 (32) <0.00001 Only observatory 4 (3) 2 (3) 0.95 χ 2 with 4×2 variables 0.00015 Autonomy in therapy prescription 120 63 Completely independent 26 (22) 10 (16) 0.35 Need of indirect supervision 58 (48) 19 (30) 0.018 Need of direct supervision 28 (23) 30 (48) 0.0008 Only observatory 8 (7) 4 (6) 0.93 χ 2 with 4×2 variables 0.009 Autonomy in electrolyte solution intravenous prescription 119 63 Completely independent 42 (35) 19 (20) 0.48 Need of indirect supervision 45 (38) 21 (33) 0.55 Need of direct supervision 23 (19) 21 (33) 0.036 Only observatory 9 (8) 2 (3) 0.24 χ 2 with 4×2 variables 0.16 Autonomy in oxygen therapy management 120 63 Completely independent 29 (24) 7 (11) 0.035 Need of indirect supervision 50 (42) 17 (27) 0.05 Need of direct supervision 34 (28) 25 (40) 0.12 Only observatory 7 (6) 14 (22) 0.00095 χ 2 with 4×2 variables 0.0006 Autonomy in managing discharges 120 62 Completely independent 24 (20) 5 (8) 0.037 Need of indirect supervision 64 (53) 26 (42) 0.14 Need of direct supervision 26 (22) 24 (39) 0.01 Only observatory 6 (5) 7 (11) 0.12 χ 2 with 4×2 variables 0.01 Autonomy in disease severity evaluation and assessment of PICU requirements 120 63 Completely independent 14 (12) 3 (5) 0.13 Need of indirect supervision 50 (42) 15 (24) 0.016 Need of direct supervision 36 (30) 33 (52) 0.003 Only observatory 20 (17) 12 (19) 0.69 χ 2 with 4×2 variables 0.009 Open in a new tab PICU, paediatric intensive care unit. In synthesis, a clear difference between the three settings emerged: In primary care, residents felt safer handling most tasks, either unsupervised or with limited supervision; this was not true in the ED or HW, where the majority of residents felt capable of handling admissions but not managing therapies, especially oxygen therapy, and especially the necessity of PICU. Autonomy perception according to year of residency programme and different programmes located in distinct Italian regions After evaluating the perceived levels of autonomy in LRTI management, we analysed the percentage of residents who believed that they could work (and therefore manage all types of patients) independently at PCP, ED and HW according to their year of residency. In the PCP, the percentage of residents who felt prepared to work without supervision increased from 15% in the first year to 50% in the second, 60% in the third, 77% in the fourth and 96% in the fifth year. Conversely, in the ED, the percentages were remarkably lower, remaining below 25% until the third year and reaching 72% in the final year. Similarly, in HW, the proportion of residents who felt ready to work autonomously was below 25% until their third year and increased to 50% by the fourth year and 83% by the fifth year. Specifically, we found the following differences according to the year and setting: ED (5/52, 10%) versus PCP (26/52, 50%) and HW (5/52, 10%) versus PCP in the second year (in both cases, p=0.0008), ED (9/64, 14%) versus PCP (38/64, 59%) and HW (15/64, 23%) versus PCP in the third year (p=0.0002 and 0.0074, respectively) and ED (25/77, 32%) versus PCP (60/77, 78%) in the fourth year (p=0.002) ( figure 1A ). Figure 1. (A) The percentage of paediatric residents that felt comfortable working in different settings by year of residency. (B–D) The percentage of residents with sufficient autonomy (as evaluated by tutors) in the different settings. The figure 1A depicts the percentage of residents who believed they had sufficient autonomy to work in the PCP, ED, and HW per year of the residency program. The chi-square test was used to compare groups. **P < 0.01 and *** P < 0.001. In the figure 1B–D, the blue line illustrates the percentage of residents by year who believed they had adequate autonomy to work in PCP (B), ED (C), and HW (D). The red line represents the percentage of residents by year, as evaluated by their tutors, who possessed sufficient autonomy to work in PCP (B), ED (C), and HW (D). ED, emergency department; HW, hospital ward; PCP, primary care paediatrics. Open in a new tab Simultaneously, we compared the percentage of residents evaluated by tutors considered fully capable of providing medical care to children with LRTI without any supervision. No statistically significant differences were observed between the autonomy perceptions of the tutors and residents, regardless of the year of training ( figure 1B–D ). We also analysed paediatric residents’ confidence in their skills and autonomy to practise in PCP, ED and HW based on their geographical region (North, Central and South Italy). This reflects the combined effect of the residency programme and organisation of care at the local level. At PCP, 66% of residents in Northern Italy felt prepared to work without supervision, compared with 64% in Central Italy and 61% in Southern Italy. Regarding ED, 32% of residents in the North, 28% in the Central region and 22% in the South believed that they had sufficient skills. For HW, 42% of residents in the North, 32% in the Central region and 33% in the South felt adequately skilled. The data remained consistent when tutors were interviewed, regardless of the setting. Both tutors and residents showed no significant differences in autonomy based on the regional area. Additionally, regional data comparisons between tutors’ evaluations and residents’ self-assessments revealed no significant differences ( online supplemental figure S1 ). The data show a clear difference between these three settings, with primary care identified as a setting residents feel they can manage more confidently even after a few years of residency; it is interesting to note that while not significant, there is a clear reduction in perceived autonomy from northern to southern Italy. Interest in an employment agreement Finally, we analysed the number of residents interested in employment agreements by year ( online supplemental figure S1 ). As expected, the number of those ready to accept contracts for working in a paediatric health facility without supervision progressively increased in the later years of the residency programmes. However, comparing the number of residents interested in employment agreements with those who felt prepared to work independently in the ED or HW showed no difference ( online supplemental figure S2 ). Discussion The ability to work autonomously under most circumstances is an essential aspect of medical training, particularly during the final years of residency. As new generations of paediatricians progress from graduates to proficient practitioners, residents undergo a gradual transition from supervised learning to autonomous practice. To the best of our knowledge, this is the first study to explore the level of autonomy among paediatric residents in managing LRTI. The Italian healthcare system is facing a severe shortage of physicians, 1 which greatly impacted the management of COVID-19 and continues to result in work overload during the winter as respiratory infections peak. Moreover, lockdown strategies have indirectly led to a rebound in severe respiratory infections. Paediatricians face a particularly challenging situation, dealing annually with a wide range of respiratory infections, from common colds to severe bronchiolitis. Paediatric residents are becoming a major component of paediatricians in Italy, comprising 20% of all working paediatricians. Despite not being considered specialists and generally working under supervision, approximately half of these residents are expected to be employed in the NHS, with a similar proportion in primary and hospital care. The shortage of physicians is most pronounced in emergency settings and small hospitals located far from large cities. Across the various settings examined, the ED consistently recorded the lowest level of self-assessed autonomy regardless of residency year. Specifically in managing LRTIs, the most challenging aspect requiring supervision was oxygen therapy management. Conversely, most residents reported feeling capable of independently obtaining medical history, conducting clinical assessments, prescribing laboratory and radiological tests and prescribing drug therapies. Our data indicated a remarkable level of uncertainty in decision-making across all settings. In the ED, residents expressed a need for supervision when determining whether a patient could be discharged or required hospitalisation. Similarly, in the HW group, residents lacked confidence in assessing patients for discharge. Limitations were also evident in assessing the severity of a patient’s condition and determining the potential need for admission to the PICU, whether the patient was in the ED or HW, or in evaluating the necessity of accessing the ED if the patient had previously been seen in an outpatient setting such as a PCP. When the same questions were asked to tutors regarding their supervised residents, no significant differences were observed. Tutors confirmed lower levels of autonomy in the ED, challenges with oxygen therapy management and difficulties in making decisions regarding patient severity, closely aligning with the residents’ assessments. Autonomy in medical education has been addressed in the literature through several theories aimed at improving residency programmes. As outlined by the Accreditation Council for Graduate Medical Education milestones, paediatric residents are required to progressively achieve competencies in patient evaluation, management and related procedural skills throughout their training. 11 Furthermore, residents must attain specific patient care and procedural skill competencies to deliver quality care that is ‘safe, effective, timely, efficient, patient-centred, equitable and designed to improve population health’. 12 Over the years, various strategies have been developed to optimise the acquisition of autonomy. According to Wielenga-Meijer, having some degree of autonomy when learning a task is preferable to having none. However, once a certain level of autonomy is reached (eg, moderate autonomy), further increases beyond that point (ie, full autonomy) may not provide additional benefits regarding motivation and learning outcomes. Moreover, excessive autonomy can potentially hinder learning efficiency. 13 An autonomy-supportive learning environment is likely to foster self-directed learning, resulting in enhanced medical knowledge and skills. 13 Regardless of the strategy adopted, the degree of autonomy perceived by the residents is crucial. Previous research has suggested that a perceived optimal level of autonomy may lead to fewer medical errors and increased support for patient autonomy. 14 , 17 Currently, competency-based training is a widely accepted model of medical education that uses an organised framework of competencies to develop entrustable professional activities (EPAs). 18 An EPA is defined as ‘a unit of professional practice that can be fully entrusted to a trainee once they have demonstrated the necessary competence to execute this activity unsupervised’. 19 In our study, to assess the overall degree of perceived autonomy, we analysed the number of paediatric residents who believed that they had adequate skills and autonomy to work in the PCP, ED or HW. The responses obtained raised substantial concerns because even at the final stages of residency, a considerable number of key respondents reported limits in self-managing common clinical cases of sick children. As expected, deficiencies in management were more pronounced in emergency settings, where limitations carry potentially serious consequences. A similar response pattern was obtained when tutors were asked whether their residents had sufficient autonomy to work alone in the PCP, ED or HW. As the questionnaires were collected independently, the similarity in the results supported their reliability. Subsequently, we investigated potential variations based on specific residency programmes or geographic locations. In general terms, the healthcare system appears to perform more effectively in Northern Italy compared with Central and Southern regions. 20 Although lower percentages of autonomy were observed in the Southern region, the differences were not statistically significant. These data indicate consistency in paediatric specialist training across different regions of Italy and indirectly suggest a similar standard of care provided nationwide in paediatric settings. Medical education and training present significant challenges in the emergency setting, where rapid decision-making is paramount, and outcomes can be irreversible. Additionally, interactions with parents are often stressful and introduce bias, requiring specific skills not commonly found among paediatric residents. Marsh et al conducted a study highlighting residents’ appreciation for collaborative learning environments that prioritise safety. They underscore the significance of intentional experiences, such as season-based learning and access to procedures, facilitated by engaging teaching faculty who offer graded supervision. 12 A recent analysis identified barriers to ED education among paediatric residents. To address these challenges, trainers and mentors should establish clear expectations and goals for rotations, foster a positive atmosphere supporting shared decision-making and grant residents autonomy to develop their practice styles. 21 Medical education studies have emphasised the importance of establishing a psychologically safe and positive learning climate for learners’ skill mastery. 22 23 These requirements for a quiet and safe learning environment contrast with those of emergency settings. Simulation-based medical training appears effective in improving both medical and non-technical skills in a controlled setting, as described by Binotti et al. 24 Given that many paediatric residents lack experience in caring for critically ill children, 25 simulation offers an ideal platform for learning through direct observation, frequent practice and constructive feedback. Simulations also provide an objective method for assessing competency attainment. 24 In our survey, we found a notable increase in the percentage of residents willing to accept employment contracts, rising from 6% initially to 72% by the final year of residency. However, these figures surpass the proportion of trainees who feel adequately skilled to work independently, particularly in ED and HW. Although residents may offer valuable support in routine healthcare settings, a significant minority (one out of four key responders in their final year of residency) perceive this approach as a potential barrier to efficiently managing children, especially in stressful conditions such as peak times of acute respiratory infections. This approach warrants careful consideration and customisation, taking into account both the competence of the residents and the operational conditions. Unfortunately, the physician shortage disproportionately affects hospitals in smaller centres, where the availability of teaching support from experienced physicians may be limited. Addressing the shortage of physicians faced by many countries, particularly in wealthier nations, requires a multifaceted approach. Epidemics of infectious diseases like COVID-19 and seasonal influenza place significant strain on healthcare systems, demanding substantial work and resources. A comprehensive strategy is necessary, which combines valuable professional resources like enhancing the skills of non-medical competencies (nurses), implementing innovative approaches like telemedicine, 26 implementing preventive measures such as immunisation programmes and establishing educational initiatives. One notable limitation of this study was the small sample size, despite efforts to boost participation. Another important limitation was its reliance on self-reported evaluations by paediatric residents, which may have introduced biases related to individual interpretations of autonomy levels. Self-reported data can be influenced by various factors, such as aspirations for social advancement and economic concerns, leading respondents to provide answers they perceive as favourable rather than reflecting their true experiences. However, the consistency in responses between residents and tutors supported the overall findings. Moreover, healthcare systems vary significantly across countries regarding infrastructure, resources, cultural factors and training programmes, all of which can influence the level of autonomy granted to medical trainees. Conclusions This study examined the perceived autonomy levels of paediatric residents within the Italian healthcare system. Despite rigorous residency programmes, many residents lacked the readiness to manage patients without supervision. This persistent lack of confidence, even among final year trainees, revealed a gap in paediatric training programmes. Addressing this issue is imperative to ensure new paediatricians possess clinical competence and confidence in autonomous patient care. These findings, especially regarding challenges in emergency settings and self-reported limitations, provide a valuable foundation for further development in the field. Supplementary material online supplemental file 1 bmjopen-15-7-s001.docx (185.5KB, docx) DOI: 10.1136/bmjopen-2024-097799 Acknowledgements We would like to express our deepest appreciation to the members of the PNRR Group and to the ONSP association for their invaluable contribution and collaborative spirit throughout this research project. Footnotes Funding: The authors have not declared a specific grant for this research from any funding agency in the public, commercial or not-for-profit sectors. Prepub: Prepublication history and additional supplemental material for this paper are available online. To view these files, please visit the journal online ( https://doi.org/10.1136/bmjopen-2024-097799 ). Provenance and peer review: Not commissioned; externally peer reviewed. Patient consent for publication: Not applicable. Data availability free text: Data are available upon reasonable request. The dataset is available on enquiry to the corresponding author. Patient and public involvement: Patients and/or the public were not involved in the design, or conduct, or reporting, or dissemination plans of this research. Data availability statement Data are available upon reasonable request. References 1. Paterlini M. Warnings over doctor shortages amid Italian health reforms. Lancet. 2022;399:S0140-6736(22)01009-1. doi: 10.1016/S0140-6736(22)01009-1. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 2. Fondazione GIMBE Comunicato stampa 04 Maggio 2023. https://coronavirus.gimbe.org/press/comunicato.it Available. 3. SISAC MEDICINA CONVENZIONATA - Numero medici al 1° gennaio 2023. https://www.sisac.info/ActionPagina_296.do Available. 4. Bando accesso medici alle scuole di specializzazione A.A. 2015/2016. http://attiministeriali.miur.it/anno-2016/maggio/dm-20052016.aspx Available. 5. Decreto Ministeriale n. 1268 del 26-09-2023. https://www.mur.gov.it/it/atti-e-normativa/decreto-ministeriale-n-1268-del-26-9-2023 Available. 6. ENPAM. https://www.enpam.it/2023/via-libera-alla-norma-tampone-medici-convenzionati Available. 7. Ghirardo S, Ullmann N, Zago A, et al. Increased bronchiolitis burden and severity after the pandemic: a national multicentric study. Ital J Pediatr. 2024;50:25. doi: 10.1186/s13052-024-01602-3. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 8. Antonino B, Emanuela G, Alberto MU, et al. La sorveglianza integrata dei virus respiratori RespiVirNet in Italia: i risultati della stagione 2023-2024. Boll Epidemiol Na