pch.ac.uk/training-examinations-professional-development/postgraduate-training/general-paediatric-training/gen . Accessed 28 July 2017. 41. https://www.rcpch.ac.uk/sites/default/files/2018-05/undergraduate_curriculum_for_child_health_nov._2015_0.pdf . Accessed 28 July 2017. 42. http://www.rcpch.ac.uk/training-examinations-professional-development/assessment-and-examinations/assessment/directly-obser Accessed 28 July 2017. 43. Lomis K, Amiel JL, Ryan MS, Esposito K, Green M, Stagnaro-Green A, Bull J, Mejicano GC. Implementing an Entrustable professional activities framework in undergraduate medical education : Early lessons from the AAMC Core Entrustable Activities for Entering Residency Pilot. Acad Med. 2017;92(6):765–770. doi: 10.1097/ACM.0000000000001543. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ]',32,'2025-10-10 08:00:25','2025-10-10 08:00:25');
INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(1304,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12336929','The Role of Social Paediatrics in the 21st Century: The Yield of 50 Years of Social Paediatrics in the Netherlands.','van de Putte EM, Berkelbach van der Sprenkel EE, Nijhof SL.','2025','myalgic decca issop exertional jacobi 1920s specialisations sidelined wilhelmina attentiveness monodisciplinary surmenage deceases divorce catastrophising noah myalgic 3x3 fitnet noah noah noah breathless slightest breathlessness noah fertilisation noah knit overburdened handicapped deconditioned catastrophising noah noah fitnet rebuilding schoolwork noah epilogue noah exertional ivabradine loos polytrauma issop deprioritise seyscentra decca wilhelmina hbsc wilhelmina momentary embodies prioritises recognises banked honouring kutzsche jacobi activist paediatrica wisk dhairyawan federatie medisch specialisten visiedocument medisch ambitie vertrouwen samenwerken demedischspecialist visiedocument 20medisch 20specialist def witjes kars e001373 bmjpo siebel heiser inhestern s41390 witjes berkelbach sprenkel kars s12887 bele chugh teela haverman werkgroep generieke kinderen adviesrapport generieke kinderen uitkomstgerichtezorg binaries uitkomstgerichtezorg documenten documenten adviesrapport generieke kinderen_versie1 adviesrapport generieke 28m 29s kinderen_versie1 vlist hoefnagels dalmeijer s10654 dorst pyuza nkurunungi s41577 nijhof bleijenberg uiterwaal kimpen putte fitnet loos rijn krug polytrauma jflm mcclafferty e20171961 peds_20171961 schaff s41390 musabyemariya braxley ghspjournal seyscentra seyscentra rijn affourtit karst kamphuis putte e031008 decca decca leck decca 2021_en kwaliteitsinstituut federatie medische specialisten kennisagenda psyche demedischspecialist kennisagenda_soma_en_psyche_jeugd_nvvp_nvk_2025 berkelbach sprenkel nijhof dalmeijer hbsc vroegindeweij levelt houtveen jpsychores vroegindeweij houtveen lucassen bjhp vroegindeweij swart houtveen qfs roodenburg nijhof e2434699 defelippe otte','ABSTRACT This article explores the relevance and future of social paediatrics, drawing on 50 years of experience in the Netherlands. Social paediatrics is a subspecialty focused on children''s health in relation to their environment, including family, school and social networks. It requires a life‐course approach and integrates preventive, curative and developmental perspectives, with a key role for the child as an active participant. The field''s importance is growing amid rising chronic conditions linked to social and behavioural determinants. By addressing these complex challenges beyond the biomedical model, social paediatrics contributes to improved long‐term outcomes for children by offering a more inclusive, preventive and person‐centred framework. Keywords: biopsychosocial model, child health, person‐centred care, social paediatrics Abbreviations ARFID Avoidant Restrictive Food Intake Disorder CFS/ME Chronic Fatigue Syndrome/Myalgic Encephalitis DECCA Dutch Expertise Centre on Child Abuse ESM Experience Sampling Methodology ISSOP International Society for Social Pediatrics and Child Health PEM post‐exertional malaise PROMs Patient Reported Outcome Measurements 1. The History and Evolution of Social Paediatrics The origins of social paediatrics date back to the early 20th century, when Dr. Abraham Jacobi, then president of the American Medical Association, emphasised the impact of environmental and social conditions on child health [ 1 ]. Since then, its relevance has only increased. Advances in medical care have improved survival rates for many previously fatal diseases, leading to a rise in chronic illnesses requiring a broad approach [ 2 ]. There is also a marked increase in conditions associated with social and behavioural factors—areas beyond the scope of a purely biomedical model. Social paediatrics provides the frameworks needed to address these complex challenges. This article outlines the scope and significance of social paediatrics and describes its development in the Netherlands. Historically, paediatrics emphasised prevention, as curative options were limited. The first paediatricians in the 1920s focused on reducing infant mortality through vaccinations and hygiene education. With medical advances, the field shifted towards specialisations like neonatology and oncology. While extremely valuable, this shift sidelined the preventive and psychosocial dimensions of care, highlighting the need for a dedicated subspecialty in paediatrics. In 1977, the Section of Social and Psychosocial Paediatrics was established—now one of 17 sections of the Dutch Society of Paediatrics. Since 2007, a formal two‐and‐a‐half‐year training programme in social paediatrics has been available in four academic centres, following 5 years of general paediatric training. Today, paediatricians, general practitioners and youth public health physicians operate across hospitals, general practices and preventive services. Social paediatric specialists collaborate with mental health professionals, child protection specialists and forensic experts, often across separately funded systems. Social paediatrics is best understood as the subspecialty that addresses children''s health in relation to their environment—including family, school and social networks. It follows a life‐course approach that integrates prevention, care and future development. The child plays a key role as an active participant with rights, developmental needs, emotions and behaviour. 2. Contemporary Relevance of Social Paediatrics Health care is shifting towards three core elements—long embedded in social paediatrics: broader health concepts, shared decision‐making and patient‐reported outcome measures (PROMs) [ 3 ]. These elements are interdependent and essential for personalised, context‐sensitive care. The need for a broader concept of health calls for a relational approach and shared decision‐making. To properly inform and evaluate this process, we need PROMs [ 4 ]. 2.1. Broad Concepts of Health The dialogue between paediatrician, child and parents is increasingly shaped by what makes life meaningful—not just disease management. This shift has an impact on decisions about treatment, referrals and psychosocial support. The concept of ‘positive health’, which emphasises resilience, adaptability and well‐being, aligns closely with social paediatrics [ 5 ]. By embracing these broader dimensions of health, paediatricians can help children realise their full potential, even in the context of chronic illness or adversity. 2.2. Shared Decision‐Making Shared decision‐making begins with identifying the child''s values and preferences, followed by exploring treatment options and their implications. Decisions are made collaboratively , aiming for the greatest positive impact [ 6 ]. Tools like the My Positive Health Dialogue Tool aid this process. It is based on interviews with ill and healthy children and encourages open discussions about all aspects related to health, such as our body, feelings and thoughts, and future aspirations [ 5 , 7 ]. This approach strengthens the relationship between healthcare professionals, children and their caregivers, making care more responsive and meaningful [ 7 ]. 2.3. Broad Outcome Measurements and PROMs Across medical specialties, there is growing recognition of the need to assess broader outcomes of care. The focus is shifting from disease‐specific endpoints to quality of life, participation and mental health. PROMs have become key tools in this transition [ 8 , 9 ]. In paediatrics, PROMs assess domains, such as quality of life, perceived health, physical, emotional and social functioning, self‐efficacy as well as symptoms. At institutions like the Wilhelmina Children''s Hospital, PROMs are routinely used to tailor care [ 10 ]. These insights into the child''s lived experience can reveal needs that might otherwise go unnoticed—for example, social isolation following illness, prompting referrals to community support. This leads to more personalised care, informed not only by biomedical indicators but also by the child''s values and preferences. Such care requires the development of a high level of trust between clinician and child. Establishing that trust is as vital as making the correct diagnosis. It depends on communication skills and an attitude of attentiveness, respect and curiosity—where care is provided alongside cure, and the child is seen as a partner [ 11 ]. Together, broad health concepts, shared decision‐making and PROMs underpin the life‐course approach of social paediatrics. This enables timely, tailored interventions for children at risk of adverse health, developmental or social outcomes, while fostering autonomy and long‐term well‐being. The principles described above align closely with those of the Dutch Section of Social and Psychosocial Paediatrics since its founding in 1977. Today, the role of the social paediatric specialist is more vital than ever: to provide this care, to lead by example and to equip other health professionals to do the same. 3. The Biopsychosocial Model in Social Paediatrics Social paediatrics is grounded in the biopsychosocial model, introduced by George Engel in 1977 [ 12 ]. Engel challenged the dominant biomedical paradigm, which separated mind and body as independent substances, and instead proposed that health conditions arise from the interaction of biological, psychological and social factors. This integrated view forms the theoretical foundation of social paediatrics, where the child is seen not merely as a clinical case, but as a developing person embedded in a dynamic social context [ 12 ]. The biopsychosocial model helps explain how various factors influence symptoms and the efficacy of treatment. For example, recent studies show that socio‐ and economic factors can impact vaccine efficacy, such as with the effectiveness of a malaria vaccine [ 13 ]. This model identifies three interconnected domains—biological, psychological and social—that collectively influence health conditions. Each domain can be further divided into predisposing, triggering and interacting factors. Predisposing factors increase vulnerability (e.g., genetic risks, trauma and poverty), triggering factors precipitate symptoms (e.g., infections, stress and life events) and interacting factors affect daily functioning (e.g., sleep, mood and family dynamics). Interacting factors can be modified while the others usually cannot. Treatment often targets these interacting factors, whether in the biological, psychological or social domain. If clinicians overlook the interplay of biological, psychological and social factors, there is a risk of inadvertently labelling complex conditions as either ‘physical’ or ‘mental’ and treating them in a monodisciplinary way, with loss of treatment effectiveness if the relevant interacting factors are not addressed. Social paediatric specialists consistently apply the biopsychosocial model to all conditions, such as inflammatory bowel disease, where flare‐ups may be influenced not only by immunological factors, but also by psychological stress, poor treatment adherence or social disruption. Social paediatric specialists use a 3 × 3 table during consultations to co‐create a shared narrative with the child and parents (Table 1 ). This narrative helps identify meaningful targets for change. It also explores the role of the family and immediate environment in shaping the child''s experience, allowing intergenerational and contextual influences to be addressed. TABLE 1. The 3 × 3 framework of the biopsychosocial model, including illustrative but non‐exhaustive examples in each cell. Biological domain—Your body Psychological domain—Your thoughts and feelings Social domain—Your environment Predisposing factors (that make you more vulnerable or resilient) – (Epi)genetic risk or protective profiles – Physical vulnerability, diseases – Personality – Character – Attachment style – Coping, skills – Increased sensitivity – Adverse childhood events – Family, friends, school, work and living environment – Family members with their illnesses – Familial interactive patterns in coping with disease Triggering factors (or prior events) – Physical stress (infections, injury and medical procedures) or surmenage (sport) – Psychological stress (examinations) – Trauma (neglect, child abuse, deceases and bullying) – Changes in social environment (school, divorce, moving and work) Interacting factors (or relevant daily factors) – Day/night rhythm – Physical exercise – Daily food – Medications – Somatosensory amplification – Mood – Anxiety – Stress – Helpful thoughts – Symptom focusing – Catastrophising thoughts – Coping and skills – Key persons (family/friends/therapist etc.) – Significance of these key persons – Most suitable environment – Required adjustments to the environment Open in a new tab For instance, in the case of Noah, a 16‐year‐old with chronic fatigue syndrome/myalgic encephalitis (CFS/ME), the 3x3 table enabled shared understanding and led to effective treatment using FITNET, a cognitive behavioural therapy for chronic fatigue (Box 1 ) [ 14 ]. More recently, personalised monitoring through experience sampling methods (ESM) and targeted therapies have further improved care [ 9 ]. BOX 1. Applying the Biopsychosocial Model: The Case of Noah, a 16‐Year‐Old Boy. Until March 2020, Noah was a healthy and active boy. At the start of the first corona pandemic, he fell ill with a fever, cough and flu‐like symptoms. The whole family was unwell. His father was hospitalised for COVID‐19 with severe shortness of breath and remained in intensive care for weeks. Noah recovered without hospitalisation, but his daily life had totally changed because of ongoing fatigue. By July 2020, he was so tired that he could no longer go to school and became breathless with chest pain at the slightest exertion (walking 400 m). In addition to fatigue and breathlessness, he suffers from headaches, joint pain and dizziness. He could not resume playing football. He was now in the 5th grade of high school and had always enjoyed school, with good results. Noah is the only child of his parents. His mother became pregnant through in vitro fertilisation at the age of 44. His twin sister was born dead. His parents have a protective style of parenting and are very worried about Noah. He has been examined by a cardiologist and has seen a physiotherapist for graduated exercises. But these interventions have not helped him at all. – Physical examination: Height and weight: SD 0. RR 121/67, heart rate 105/min. – Diagnostics (only relevant results are shown): SARS‐CoV‐2 antibodies present; cardiologist: echocardiography and ECG: no abnormalities. – Diagnosis: CFS following COVID‐19 infection, later termed post‐COVID. Biological—Your body Psychological—Your thoughts and feelings Social—Your environment Predisposing factors – Prior: good physical health – Adverse childhood events in the family (death of this twin sister) – Close‐knit club of friends – Protective (older) parents – Overburdened mother (2 sick family members) Triggering factors – COVID‐19 infection prior to first complaints – Psychological stress (worries about school; worries about his father being severely handicapped after ICU attendance for COVID‐19) – Fills his day with playing the piano – Little contact with school, no school attendance Interacting factors – Healthy food intake – No sleep disturbances – Deconditioned – Pain on his chest – Dizziness – Dysfunctional breathing – Reflective – Goal oriented – Symptom focusing – Catastrophising thoughts (‘I will never be cured’) – Contact with peers – Little autonomy – Feels dependent on his (protective) parents Open in a new tab In a dialogue with Noah and his parents, it became clear that Noah was eager to address the psychological factors that were now affecting his recovery. He started cognitive behavioural therapy via FITNET [ 14 ]. It took 6 months for him to gain more confidence and to cautiously begin rebuilding his fitness. During those 6 months, schoolwork was still barely possible. In the second 6 months, he started school again and passed his final examinations after 2 years. His mother was temporarily supported by extra staff in the home. She followed a parallel counselling programme in which she was given suggestions on how to support Noah to develop more autonomy and agency. Professionals involved: social paediatric specialist, social worker, cognitive behavioural psychologist, physiotherapist, youth public health physician (school). Epilogue: In 2025, Noah would have been referred to the expertise clinic for children with Post‐COVID (three locations in the Netherlands). Extensive measurements (4 weeks) with wearables and a smartphone would partially clarify his limitations, showing dysregulation in heart rate and variability (orthostatic intolerance) and post‐exertional malaise (PEM). He is advised to improve his salt‐ and fluid intake and to pace his activities. If there is no improvement in 2 months, options include pharmaceutical treatment within a n = 1 study design with a drug targeting orthostatic intolerance (propranolol or ivabradine) [ 28 ]. The biopsychosocial model remains relevant even in acute settings illustrated with the findings in the study of Loos, showing the prevalence of non‐accidental trauma among children with polytrauma [ 15 ]. A child arriving in the emergency department may be coping with family conflict, or fear—factors that affect symptom perception, coping and recovery. Using a biopsychosocial lens helps identify such stressors and shape care plans that go beyond biomedical treatment to restore control and confidence. Even in urgent situations, where clinical action is paramount, the principles of trust, communication and partnership remain vital. This approach supports shared decision‐making and co‐ownership between doctor, child and caregivers, adapted to the clinical setting and the child''s developmental stage. Recognising and addressing the psychosocial context during high‐stress moments can positively affect outcomes and reinforce a respectful, child‐centred model of acute care. 4. Global Perspective on the Concept of Social Paediatrics The holistic approach of social paediatrics aligns with several international models, including integrative paediatrics [ 16 ], narrative‐based paediatrics [ 17 ], developmental paediatrics, community paediatrics and adolescent medicine. The International Society for Social Pediatrics and Child Health (ISSOP), founded in Europe in 1977, connects professionals globally. However, implementing PROMs and shared decision‐making in diverse healthcare systems poses challenges. These are rooted in Western concepts of autonomy and patient rights, which may not align with cultural norms elsewhere. Systemic barriers also exist—such as limited time, staffing or infrastructure—which can deprioritise these approaches compared with acute care demands [ 18 ]. Addressing both cultural and logistical barriers is crucial for equitable global application. 5. Expertise and Specialised Areas in Social Paediatrics In the Netherlands, all social paediatric specialists practise as general paediatricians using a biopsychosocial lens to address complex cases involving medical and social challenges. In addition, they provide national expertise in three key areas: 5.1. Eating Disorders Conditions like avoidant/restrictive food intake disorder (ARFID) are managed in multidisciplinary centres, such as SeysCentra, which integrate medical, psychological and social care [ 19 , 15 ]. 5.2. Child Abuse Specialists support timely, expert assessment via institutions like the Dutch Expertise Centre on Child Abuse (DECCA), working closely with forensic doctors and social services [ 20 , 21 ]. 5.3. Persistent Physical Symptoms In academic centres, they lead care for conditions, such as CFS/ME and paediatric post‐COVID, using innovative diagnostic and treatment strategies. Because of this expertise, social paediatric specialists contribute to national guidelines, training and research, shaping practice beyond individual patient care. They also identify knowledge gaps in these fields and drive research to close them. Their role bridges disciplines, ensuring care is not only medically sound and evidence‐based but also socially responsive. Through education, advocacy and interdisciplinary collaboration, they advance paediatric care for children facing complex and often under‐recognised health challenges [ 22 ]. 6. Research in Social Paediatrics In addition to research focused on the above areas of expertise, the research is connected to the three core elements of social paediatrics and its underlying biopsychosocial model. PROM‐based cohorts of chronically ill children combined with risk‐based intervention strategies provide evidence of the effectiveness of early, personalised interventions and form the basis of life‐course medicine. The PROMs should align with questionnaires in children''s population to distinguish disease‐specific outcomes from typical developmental patterns. The PROactive cohort at Wilhelmina Children''s Hospital, launched in 2016, aligns its measures with the international Health Behaviour in School‐aged Children (HBSC) study [ 10 , 23 ]. Regarding research into the effectiveness of treatment, a key challenge is identifying which interacting factors contribute most to disabling symptoms in individual patients. At Wilhelmina Children''s Hospital, we developed smartphone‐based Ecological Momentary Assessment (EMA) to track real‐time interactions between symptoms and daily experiences in conditions, such as CFS/ME, Q‐Fever Fatigue Syndrome, Juvenile Idiopathic Arthritis and post‐COVID [ 24 , 25 ]. To evaluate personalised treatments, single‐case ( n = 1) trials have been developed and are now used to assess off‐label symptom treatments in expert centres [ 26 ]. Meta‐analyses of these trials may offer broader insights into treatment effectiveness across similar patient groups. To further embed the biopsychosocial model in practice, implementation research is essential. This includes studying barriers and facilitators for integrating PROMs, shared decision‐making and personalised care. Our work on the My Positive Health Dialogue Tool exemplifies such implementation efforts [ 7 ]. Together, these research strategies advance social paediatrics as both a clinical and scientific discipline, ensuring its principles are embedded in everyday care and policy. 7. Future Directions of Social Paediatrics The increasing complexity of modern paediatric healthcare necessitates a paradigm shift from a purely biomedical approach to one that embraces the whole child—its needs, aspirations and future. Social paediatrics embodies this perspective, integrating biological, psychological and social dimensions of health to ensure care is not only medically effective but also meaningful and relevant to children''s daily lives. Social paediatrics sets an example and can support this transformation by offering pathways to more inclusive, effective and compassionate child health care. In the end, all paediatricians will have to play a crucial role in improving outcomes by incorporating PROMs, engaging in shared decision‐making with children and families, and focusing on what truly adds value to life. This approach fosters mutual trust, offering deeper insight into lived experiences and empowering families throughout the care process. To provide truly personalised care, we must move closer to the child''s environment—its thoughts, feelings and beliefs—ensuring that care not only treats disease but supports well‐being. The future of paediatrics lies in a holistic model that prioritises the child''s voice, recognises individual needs and promotes long‐term health. This transformation will require continued input from clinical research. For instance, recent research has shown that many children with chronic conditions have elevated cardiovascular and psychosocial risks that could be influenced by disease factors as well as elevated stress or social disadvantage [ 27 ]. The translation from research into personalised care demands a systems‐level approach—integrating clinical insights, longitudinal harmonised cohort data and bio‐banked biological markers to identify risk early and deliver timely, targeted interventions. Responding to these risks requires not only appropriate research and early recognition strategies but also interdisciplinary interventions embedded in the child''s daily environment. The transformation also requires a willingness to overcome systemic barriers. It is essential that these barriers, such as separate funding systems for medical and mental health care, are addressed by implementing incentives that will drive the transformation. Strong partnerships across health care, education, research and policy are crucial. Only by honouring each child''s complexity—across medical, emotional and relational domains—can health care truly support lifelong well‐being. With its preventive ethos and integrative mindset, social paediatrics can play a key role in supporting this transformation. Social paediatrics should evolve as a scientific and educational discipline that strengthens all of paediatric care. Its principles should be embedded across training, practice and research. In parallel, the field must continue to generate and translate scientific insights into policy and prevention strategies. Conflicts of Interest The authors declare no conflicts of interest. Funding: The authors received no specific funding for this work. Data Availability Statement Data sharing not applicable to this article as no datasets were generated or analysed during the current study. References 1. Kutzsche S., “Abraham Jacobi (1830–1919) and His Transition From Political to Medical Activist,” Acta Paediatrica 110, no. 8 (2021): 2303–2305, 10.1111/apa.15887. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 2. Wisk L. E. and Sharma N., “Prevalence and Trends in Pediatric‐Onset Chronic Conditions in the United States, 1999–2018,” Academic Pediatrics 25, no. 4 (2025): 102810, 10.1016/j.acap.2025.102810. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 3. Dhairyawan R., “Reflect, Collaborate, and Listen,” Lancet 405, no. 10478 (2025): 536–537, 10.1016/S0140-6736(25)00251-X. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 4. Federatie Medisch Specialisten , “Visiedocument Medisch Specialist 2025: Ambitie, Vertrouwen, Samenwerken,” 2025, accessed May 14, 2025, https://demedischspecialist.nl/sites/default/files/Visiedocument%20Medisch%20Specialist%202025‐DEF.pdf . 5. de Jong‐Witjes S., Kars M. C., van Vliet M., et al., “Development of the My Positive Health Dialogue Tool for Children: A Qualitative Study on Children''s Views of Health,” BMJ Paediatr Open 6, no. 1 (2022): e001373, 10.1136/bmjpo-2021-001373. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 6. Bosch I., Siebel H., Heiser M., and Inhestern L., “Decision‐Making for Children and Adolescents: A Scoping Review of Interventions Increasing Participation in Decision‐Making,” Pediatric Research 97 (2025): 1840–1854, 10.1038/s41390-024-03509-5. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 7. de Jong‐Witjes S., van der Berkelbach Sprenkel E. E., Kars M. C., et al., “Implementing the My Positive Health Dialogue Tool for Children With a Chronic Condition: Barriers and Facilitators,” BMC Pediatrics 25, no. 1 (2025): 168, 10.1186/s12887-024-05258-0. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 8. Bele S., Chugh A., Mohamed B., Teela L., Haverman L., and Santana M. J., “Patient‐Reported Outcome Measures in Routine Pediatric Clinical Care: A Systematic Review,” Frontiers in Pediatrics 8 (2020): 1–11, 10.3389/fped.2020.00364. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 9. Werkgroep Generieke PRO(M)s kinderen , “Adviesrapport Set Gene