INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(1320,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC1544481','The community paediatrician in an integrated child health service.','Davis JA, Bamford FN.','1975','handicapped connexion proboems tb02119','Abstract A concept of Community Paediatrics has been described comprising clinical care to individual children including identification, diagnosis, and care of the handicapped child, expertise in preventive paediatric techniques, and the provision of investigation and advice in connexion with social and educational proboems. Its relationship to Community Medicine, General Practice, and Consulting Paediatrics has been discussed and a long-term training scheme is outlined. In the immediate future the deployment is envisaged of senior clinical Medical Officers now employed by Local Authorities. Full text PDF Previous 1 2 3 Next Selected References These references are in PubMed. This may not be the complete list of references from this article. Court S. D. Child health in a changing community. Br Med J. 1971 Apr 17;2(5754):125–131. doi: 10.1136/bmj.2.5754.125. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] Walker J. H. The future of paediatric care in Britain. The future of community paediatric care. Br J Med Educ. 1969 Dec;3(4):316–318. doi: 10.1111/j.1365-2923.1969.tb02119.x. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ]',5,'2025-10-10 08:03:53','2025-10-10 08:03:53');
INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(1321,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6794819','Patients'' and parents'' percetion of care on a paediatric interprofessional training ward.','Straub C, Bode SFN.','2019','itws itws itw itw ipapaed ipapaed ipapaed itw itw evauation cews handing detrimentally unfounded monoprofessional cews itws itw commended itws itws monoprofessional cew itws itw institutet itw itw ipapaed until18 ipapaed ipapaed itw ipapaed ipapaed ipapaed itw ipapaed ipapaed ipapaed monoprofessional ipapaed ipapaed ipapaed ipapaed ipapaed ipapaed ipapaed ipapaed formalizes ipapaed jolla ipapaed ipapaed ipapaed ipapaed ipapaed ipapaed ipapaed ipapaed ipapaed itw ipapaed monoprofessional ctw itws itw hinting commended itw ipapaed ipapaed ipapaed monoprofessional cew cew ipapaed monoprofessional cews ipapaed ipapaed praised interprofessionally ipapaed ipapaed ipapaed improvable monoprofessional ctws itws itw ipapaed ipapaed itw itw ipw itw itw itws ipapaed ipapaed cew ipapaed itw sfnb ipapaed a381 ma01 ipapaed ipapaed formalizes ipapaed straub straub uniklinik uniklinik oosterom scheffer freeth lindblom hallin e26 wynne scheffer scheffer ponzer hylin morison straub duerkop sfn scacco iecrm0045 scheffer aktive partizipation machbarkeitsstudie ausbildungsstation ausbild raes ausbild doc49 zma000941 bleakley bligh hmr 0b013e3181afc10c','Abstract Background Interprofessional training wards (ITWs) have been established in different fields of adult medicine to promote interprofessional learning and interprofessional collaboration of health care profession students. High patient satisfaction rates have been reported for ITWs. No data of parents’ and especially patients’ evaluation of care on a paediatric ITW have been reported so far. This study aims to evaluate parents’ and patients’ perceptions of medical and nursing care on a paediatric ITW. Methods In 2017 we established and started an interprofessional training ward in the setting of a general paediatric ward (IPAPAED). Medical students and nurse trainees care for 4–6 patients under supervision of registered nurses and certified physicians. All parents and all patients older than 8 years were invited to evaluate different aspect of their care on the IPAPAED. Results Since November 2017 until February 2019 parents ( n = 109) rated the overall care of their children on the IPAPAED ward with m = 1.21 (SD ± .43) (1 = “excellent”, 4 = “poor”). Patients ( n = 56) rated their overall care with m = 1.29 (SD ± 0.5). Other aspects of care and interprofessional collaboration were rated equally well. Analysis of the (limited) free-text commentaries revealed that perceived quality of care, friendliness and communication were especially valued by patients and parents. Discussion & conclusion On a paediatric ITW, in the view of parents and patients in our sample, a high level of care is delivered and satisfaction rates are excellent. An ITW seems, from a patient and parent point of view, feasible, even in paediatrics. Keywords: Interprofessional training ward, Patients’ evauation, Parental evaluation, Medical education, Interprofessional learning, Interprofessional collaboration Background Challenges in today’s complex healthcare should be dealt with by an interprofessional team to ensure optimal patient outcomes [ 1 ]. Clinical education wards (CEWs) are educational interventions to promote active learning of students of different health care professions from, with, and about each other by involving them directly in patient care and allowing them to take on responsibility for the care of patients under supervision by experienced health-care professionals to prepare them for their future working together as an interprofessional team [ 2 , 3 ]. There were fears that this handing over of responsibility could detrimentally affect care [ 3 ]. The feasibility of students taking on responsibility for patient care has been demonstrated extensively as have positive effects on professional role development, interprofessional competencies, patient outcomes and cost effectiveness [ 3 – 14 ]. Also concerns that allowing students to care nearly independently for patients might not be accepted by patients have been shown to be unfounded as both monoprofessional CEWs for medical students have reported positive patient perceptions [ 3 , 12 ] as have interprofessional training wards (ITWs) [ 4 – 10 ]. In some cases patient satisfaction was even higher on ITW wards compared to conventional wards [ 5 , 8 ]. Students’ empathy, understandable communication, personal care, enthusiasm, and motivation were especially commended by patients [ 3 – 10 ]. On ITWs, medical students and nursing students, sometimes also other allied health professional students, work and learn together and about each other [ 1 ]. Reported ITWs were mainly established in the setting of orthopaedic wards as well as in other areas of adult medicine [ 4 – 10 ]. Data on parental evaluation exist for a paediatric monoprofessional CEW [ 3 ]. Overall satisfaction was high and no differences in medical treatment success, complications rates, and overall care could be identified when compared to a control group in the same, as well as other hospitals [ 3 ]. This supports the concept that medical students can, under appropriate supervision, take care of paediatric patients with no negative effects on both quality of care and parents’ perception of care. To our knowledge no patient satisfaction and evaluation data have been reported from paediatric ITWs so far. Neither the paediatric ITW that existed in Stockholm, nor the paediatric interprofessional emergency department at Karolinska Institutet in Stockholm, Sweden, have published data, nor are patients/parent evaluation data reported from an ITW-like setting that existed in Ireland [ 15 ]. Therefore there is a need to investigate how care on a paediatric ITW is perceived by patients and their families if they are cared for primarily by nurses and physicians in training. The paediatric setting poses special challenges in general, but especially for participants, including age-appropriate communication with both patients and parents, a wide age diversity, and a plethora of different clinical pictures. In November 2017 we established an interprofessional education ward in the setting of a general paediatric ward (IPAPAED). Here, medical students and nurse trainees work together for 2 weeks in teams of two medical students and two nurse trainees, both in their final year. As interprofessional teams they take on responsibility for the care of patients from the end of the first year until18 years of age. The IPAPAED is integrated in a general paediatric ward and the team takes care of four to six patients. Nurses and physicians in training are supervised by registered nurses and certified medical doctors as described elsewhere [ 16 ]. As nurse trainees alter between practical and theoretical “education blocks” of 4 weeks, the IPAPAED ITW takes place non-continuously for “blocks” of 4 weeks each (Fig. 1 ). Evaluation results are reported for the blocks in November 2017, February 2018, May/June 2018, October/November 2018, and February 2019. In between the “blocks” nurse trainees in the first 2 years of their course, and medical students in their last year are routinely involved in patient care but they do not receive interprofessional preparation and supervision. Fig. 1. Open in a new tab IPAPAED ward setup All participants of the IPAPAED are specifically prepared for interprofessional teamwork, communication, feedback, and ward specific activities in advance. Special emphasis is put on patient-centered care and participants are instructed to include patients and their families as far as possible in decision making and care planning while on the IPAPAED ward. The ITW rotation on the IPAPAED is the first chance for all participants to actually be the first professional contact persons for patients and their families during their hospital stay. Specific aims were to prepare the nurses and physicians in training for their later role as members of interprofessional teams. Participants are supervised by experienced nurses and physicians who receive specific training on interprofessional teaching. They ensure that both nurses and physicians in training bring their expertise into patient care, supervise feedback, help with time management, and provide a safety net for participants of the IPAPAED. Supervisors use a specifically designed form to identify aspects that went well as well as areas that needed to be improved, and to identify possible errors in treatment [ 16 ]. Methods We developed a questionnaire regarding general aspects of care and rating of the IPAPAED ward based on an example from a monoprofessional training ward [ 11 ]. Our questionnaire comprised of the following questions: Please rate the overall care you received during your hospital stay on the IPAPAED ward (1 = excellent, 2 = good, 3 = fair, 4 = poor). Please rate the impact that nurse trainees had on the care you received (1 = very positive, 2 = positive, 3 = no effect, 4 = slightly negative, 5 = negative). Please rate the impact that medical students had on the care you received (1 = very positive, 2 = positive, 3 = no effect, 4 = slightly negative, 5 = negative). Please rate the collaboration of nurses in training and medical students (1 = excellent, 2 = good, 3 = fair, 4 = poor). Did you receive all relevant information that you needed from the IPAPAED team (1 = definitely yes, 2 = yes, 3 = rather yes, 4 = rather not, 5 = not at all)? If you/your child had to be treated as an inpatient again – would you agree to be cared for on the IPAPAED again (1 = definitely, 2 = yes, 3 = rather yes, 4 = rather not, 5 = not at all)? Please note any positive comments that you might have. Please let us know what you think that needs to be changed regarding the IPAPAED. Two additional questions regarding the length of stay and the age of the patient were also included. The questionnaire was handed out to all parents of patients treated on the IPAPAED ward and all patients older than 8 years from November 2017 to February 2019. An information leaflet explaining the IPAPAED ward was handed out together with the questionnaire. Questionnaires were returned anonymously by being posted in a specifically designated letter box on the ward. Ethics The study was approved by the University of Freiburg ethics committee (permit no. 561/17). As asked by the ethics committee we were not allowed to record the gender and the exact age of the patients but had to provide age-spans for participants to tick in boxes as appropriate. The ethics committee stated that written consent was not needed to be treated on the IPAPAED as both medical students and nurse trainees in the past already routinely take care of patients on regular paediatric wards under supervision of experienced nurses and doctors as this is part of their respective training. The IPAPAED ward formalizes this supervision and gives participants the chance to take on responsibility for patient care but nurses and physicians are always on the ward to ensure optimal quality of care. Verbal consent to be treated by the IPAPAED team was asked of all patients or parents and all were given the opportunity to refrain from participating in the study – but none chose to do so. Statistical analysis Quantitative data were analysed with Graph Pad Prism version 7.01 (GraphPad Software, La Jolla, California, USA). The Mann-Whitney-U-Test was used to identify differences in parental or patients’ evaluation or between items of the questionnaire. Quantitative content analysis was used to analyse the free-text commentaries [ 17 ]. Results From November 2017 until February 2019 five IPAPAED blocks of 4 weeks (each two teams for 2 weeks, Fig. 1 ) took place and 180 patients were cared for by a total 20 nurses in training and 20 physicians in training. Mean length of hospital stay was 2.9 (SD ± .4) days. A total of 109 parents (response rate 60.5%) and 56 patients (31% of total patients, 55 patients (94.8%) > 8 years) participated in the study. Patients’ age can be found in Table 1 . Table 1. Age distribution of parents’ children and patients who participated in the evaluation Age span Parents’ evaluation Patients’ evaluation 1–3 years 12 0 4–7 years 37 1 8–10 years 18 8 11–13 years 24 24 14–16 years 15 15 17–18 years 1 4 n/a 2 4 Open in a new tab n/a not available Quantitative feedback Parents rated the overall care their children received on the IPAPAED ward with m = 1.21 (SD ± .43). Patients rated their overall care with m = 1.29 (SD ± .5). Care by nurses and physicians in training was rated equally good by parents and patients as was perceived collaboration between professions as well as information sharing (Table 2 ). Two patients stated (one 11–13 years, one 17–18 years) that they “rather not” received all the information they needed. No significant differences in the evaluation of the different items by either patients or parents were found. Table 2. Evaluation of different items by parents and patients parents Patients Impact of nurses in training on overall care ( n = 109) m = 1.35, SD ± .6 ( n = 55) m = 1.42, SD ± .64 Impact of physicians in training on overall care ( n = 109) m = 1.36, SD ± .59 ( n = 56) m = 1.41, SD ± .63 Interprofessional collaboration ( n = 103) m = 1.39, SD ± .49 ( n = 56) m = 1.38, SD ± .53 Information sharing ( n = 109) m = 1.4, SD ± .53 ( n = 53) m = 1.65, SD ± .75 Open in a new tab n number of participants for the respective item, m mean, SD standard deviation 98% of parents ( n = 107) and 96% of patients ( n = 53), who answered the respective question, stated that they would “definitely agree” or “agree” to be treated on the IPAPAED again, if another inpatient stay was needed. The remaining participants stated that they would “rather agree” to be treated on the IPAPAED again. The willingness to be treated on the IPAPAED ward again was significantly correlated with appraisal of overall care (Spearman’s r for parents: r = .43, for patients: r = .52) and perceived quality of interprofessional collaboration (parents: r = .47, patients: r = .71) in both parents as well as patients ( p < .001 for all). Free-text comments Positive comments ( n = 81) of parents mostly related to both well perceived interpersonal and communicative competencies like friendly personnel ( n = 23, e.g. “the whole team was very friendly and helpful”), information sharing ( n = 13, e.g. “very competent and transparent delivery of information”) and communication ( n = 13, e.g. “helpful discussion during ward rounds with all medical students, nurses in training, physicians, and nurses”) as well as good quality of overall care ( n = 21, e.g. “excellent care”). Twenty-seven comments just stated that everything was seen as positive. Seventeen critical comments were given by parents, of those four were IPAPAED related namely the wish for more experienced doctors ( n = 2, e.g. “maybe an experienced doctor should accompany students more frequently”), wish for more information about the program ( n = 1) and for faster working on the ward ( n = 1). All other comments concerned non-IPAPAED related items, most frequently the quality of rooms ( n = 5) and food ( n = 2) on the ward. Forty patients gave positive comments, most frequently related to the friendliness of the personal ( n = 10, e.g. “everyone was always very friendly”) and the overall care ( n = 9, e.g. “great overall care”) as well as the communication ( n = 4, e.g. “if I had questions everything was explained very well”). Critical free-text comments of patients ( n = 11) mainly targeted the quality of food ( n = 6) but no IPAPAED-related aspects. Discussion The overall aim of the IPAPAED ward is to enhance interprofessional learning and interprofessional collaboration to prepare doctors and nurses in training for their later work as members of interprofessional teams. The work presented here aims to evaluate both patients’ and parents’ perceptions of overall, medical and nursing care on a pediatric ITW. These patient perceptions of the care on the IPAPAED ward are an important factor regarding acceptance of such an educational intervention by parents, patients and health care professionals. The data presented here, to our knowledge, is the first assessment of an interprofessional education ward in a paediatric setting from a parental and paediatric patients‘perspective. The overall feedback by parents and patients of all age-groups alike was overwhelmingly positive – both in the quantitative evaluation as well as in the free-text comments. Parents showed a higher response rate than patients, this might be due to younger children not filling out or not being able to fill out in the evaluation. Similar positive parental evaluation data have been reported for a monoprofessional paediatric CTW where medical students take care of paediatric patients and a similar perceived level of care compared to a normal paediatric ward was found [ 3 ]. However this work does not report paediatric patients’ assessments of their hospital stay in contrast to the study presented here. Different studies have shown that patients’ evaluation results and standard of care were at least similar in both ITWs and conventional wards in adult medicine settings, mostly in orthopaedic wards, but also emergency, internal medicine, neurology, and geriatric settings [ 4 – 7 , 10 ] and patient satisfaction was even higher on some ITW wards hinting at a probable halo effect of the interprofessional approach itself [ 4 , 5 , 8 ]. Patients especially commended the communication with and information they received by students, more individual attention by the interprofessional teams, and that they felt that students were very motivated and enthusiastic to work on the ITW [ 4 – 7 , 9 , 10 ]. Our free-text results demonstrate that both patients and parents on the IPAPAED ward appreciated the exact same characteristics in our nurses and physicians in training. Appreciation of both interpersonal and communicative competencies but also a professional level of care, as found in the limited sample of free-text patient and parent feedback, are reassuring. Maybe the best marker for the perceived high quality of care on the IPAPAED is that 96% of patients and 98% of parents stated that they would agree to be treated on the IPAPAED ward again if needed. These results are even better than for one monoprofessional internal medicine CEW that reported that 87.5% of patients agreed to be treated on the CEW again [ 18 ]. Being responsible for patient care for the first time motivated the participating nurses and physicians in training to deliver excellent care. The main difference to other senior students on the IPAPAED is that the interprofessional responsibility for the patients as a team is actively encouraged and expected. Traditional education on clinical wards depends on the willingness of nurse and physician supervisors to hand over responsibility to trainees and students and is far from standardized in Germany even though suggestions on standardizing at least the last year of medical school exist [ 19 ]. This active participation of nurses and physicians in training leads to a direct relationship of students with their patients which seems to be a pivotal factor for the success of both monoprofessional and interprofessional CEWs and is essential for patient-centered care but a halo effect of more structured mono- or interprofessional training cannot be excluded [ 3 , 4 , 20 ]. It might be that due to high motivation to perform and excellent communication within the interprofessional team as well as with patients and parents no treatment errors occurred on the IPAPAED so far – even with nurses and doctors just at the beginning of their careers. Both nurse and physician trainees on the IPAPAED were equally valued in the evaluation and the interprofessional teamwork was praised. This hints at a high motivation to deliver the best care possible in both groups of trainees and that the teams are perceived as working together interprofessionally. The interprofessional setting itself has been attributed to higher patient satisfaction [ 8 ] and the same might hold true for the IPAPAED ward. Nurses and physicians in training have a “safety net” of experienced registered nurses and certified physicians who are on the ward at all times during their rotation on the IPAPAED. These supervisors are an important part of their team and their presence, even if only in the background, and sometimes not in the same room, might be reassuring for patients as well [ 8 ]. The supervisors ensured that the IPAPAED team discussed the patients and their individual goals or changes to therapy before entering the patient rooms and gave input to formulate those, if needed. They helped to initiate the participation of patients and parents in the decision making process, if needed. So a specific care and treatment plan was formulated beforehand as it has been described before [ 8 ]. After the ward rounds in the patient room the team gathered again with the supervisors for a short reflection of the patient interaction to highlight positive aspects, identify improvable areas, and to discuss the individual patient’s resources from a nursing and medical point of view. This setting was chosen to establish a group culture of team work [ 21 ] and that all involved persons always had the same level of information regarding the individual patients. Considering positive evaluation data from monoprofessional paediatric CTWs [ 3 , 12 ] and ITWs in other fields of medicine than paediatrics [ 4 – 10 ], our data suggest that an ITW in paediatrics is well received by patients and their parents. The care that nurses and doctors in training deliver is perceived as excellent on the IPAPAED ward. Limitations and strengths This study is limited by the lack of a control group of patients and parents. Standard patient feedback forms were available on the ward at all times but participation rates are traditionally low and feedback mainly concerns perceived low quality of the food and rooms. As the questionnaire employed for the IPAPAED ward is specifically aimed at assessment of interprofessional care and is longer than the standard questionnaire we chose not to employ it on the regular ward. All data reported are subjective impressions and no objective quality parameters (e.g. readmission rates) have been recorded. The specific focus on patient-centered care, motivated nurses and physicians in training and supervision by experienced registered nurses and certified doctors may contribute to the overall positive evaluation. The strength of this study is that it is the first to report a paediatric patients’ evaluation of an ITW and shows that both patients and parents equally appreciate the care received on a paediatric ITW. This is encouraging for others who might be in the process of starting a paediatric IPW. Conclusions From a parent as well as a patient perspective the nursing and medical care on a paediatric ITW is perceived as being excellent and no adverse opinions arose from either parents or patients regarding the care on the paediatric ITW. Both groups of trainees seem to contribute equally to the care in the view of parents and patients. Especially friendliness, quality of care, and communicative competencies are well received but the data stem from a limited sample of free-text commentaries. Our data support the implementation of ITWs even in the complex and specific setting of paediatrics. Acknowledgements We thank all patients and parents who participated in the evaluation. We also thank all IPAPAED participants, the IPAPAED supervisors, and the whole team of the ward. Abbreviations CEW Clinical education ward IPAPAED Interprofessional training ward in the setting of a general paediatric ward ITW Interprofessional training ward n number SD Standard deviation Author’s contributions CS and SFNB designed the study, analyzed the data and wrote up the manuscript. Both authors read and approved the final manuscript. Funding The implementation of the IPAPAED was financially supported by the Robert-Bosch-Foundation (“Operation Team”), funding number: 32.5.A381.0030/MA01. Availability of data and materials The datasets used and/or analyzed during the current study are available from the corresponding author on request. Ethics approval and consent to participate The study was approved by the local ethics committee (permit no. 561/17). The ethics committee stated that written consent was not needed to be treated on the IPAPAED as both medical students and nurse trainees in the past already routinely take care of patients on regular paediatric wards under supervision of experienced nurses and doctors as this is part of their respective training. The IPAPAED ward formalizes this supervision and gives participants the chance to take on responsibility for patient care but nurses and physicians are always on the ward to ensure optimal quality of care. Verbal consent to be treated by the IPAPAED team was asked of all patients or parents and all were given the opportunity to refrain from participating in the study – but none chose to do so. Consent for publication All participants consented to their evaluation data being used anonymously. Competing interests The authors declare that they have no competing interests. Footnotes Publisher’s Note Springer Nature remains neutral with regard to jurisdictional claims in published maps and institutional affiliations. Contributor Information Christine Straub, Email: Christine.Straub@uniklinik-freiburg.de. Sebastian F. N. Bode, Email: Sebastian.Bode@uniklinik-freiburg.de References 1. WHO . Framework for action on interprofessional education and collaborative practice. 2010. p. 56. [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 2. Oosterom N, et al. A review of interprofessional training wards: Enhancing student learning and patient outcomes. Med Teach. 2019;41(5):547–54. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] 3. Scheffer C, et al. Students with an autonomous role in hospital care - patients perceptions. Med Teach. 2018;40(9):944–52 [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] 4. Freeth D, et al. ‘Real life’ clinical learning on an interprofessional training ward. Nurse Educ Today. 2001;21(5):366–372. doi: 10.1054/nedt.2001.0567. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 5. Reeves S, et al. ‘It teaches you what to expect in future … ‘: interprofessional learning on a training ward for medical, nursing, occupational therapy and physiotherapy students. Med Educ. 2002;36(4):337–344. doi: 10.1046/j.1365-2923.2002.01169.x. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 6. Lindblom P, et al. Learning orthopaedics: assessing medical students’ experiences of interprofessional training in an orthopaedic clinical education ward. J Interprof Care. 2007;21(4):413–423. doi: 10.1080/13561820701401346. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 7. Hansen TB, Jacobsen F, Larsen K. Cost effective interprofessional training: an evaluation of a training unit in Denmark. J Interprof Care. 2009;23(3):234–241. doi: 10.1080/13561820802602420. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 8. Hallin K, et al. Effects of interprofessional education on patient perceived quality of care. Med Teach. 2011;33(1):e22–e26. doi: 10.3109/0142159X.2011.530314. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 9. Brewer ML, Stewart-Wynne EG. An Australian hospital-based student training ward delivering safe, client-centred care while developing students’ interprofessional practice capabilities. J Interprof Care. 2013;27(6):482–488. doi: 10.3109/13561820.2013.811639. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 10. Meek R, et al. Effect of interprofessional student-led beds on emergency department performance indicators. Emerg Med Australas. 2013;25(5):427–434. doi: 10.1111/1742-6723.12118. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 11. Scheffer C, et al. Can final year medical students significantly contribute to patient care? A pilot study about the perception of patients and clinical staff. Med Teach. 2010;32(7):552–557. doi: 10.3109/01421590903437170. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 12. Scheffer C, et al. Active student participation may enhance patient centeredness: patients'' assessments of the clinical education ward for integrative medicine. Evid Based Complement Alternat Med. 2013;2013:743832. doi: 10.1155/2013/743832. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 13. Ponzer S, et al. Interprofessional training in the context of clinical practice: goals and students'' perceptions on clinical education wards. Med Educ. 2004;38(7):727–736. doi: 10.1111/j.1365-2929.2004.01848.x. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 14. Hylin U, et al. Interprofessional training in clinical practice on a training ward for healthcare students: a two-year follow-up. J Interprof Care. 2007;21(3):277–288. doi: 10.1080/13561820601095800. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 15. Morison S, Jenkins J. Sustained effects of interprofessional shared learning on student attitudes to communication and team working depend on shared learning opportunities on clinical placement as well as in the classroom. Med Teach. 2007;29(5):464–470. doi: 10.1080/01421590701477381. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 16. Straub C, Duerkop A, Bode SFN. Learning guidance on an interprofessional training ward. J Nursing Educ Patient Educ Train. 2019. accepted for publication, 2019. 17. Coe K, Scacco JM. Content Analysis, Quantitative, in the International Encyclopedia of Communication Research Methods. Hoboken: Wiley; 2017. 10.1002/9781118901731.iecrm0045. 18. Scheffer C, et al. Lernen durch aktive Partizipation in der klinischen Patientenversorgung - Machbarkeitsstudie einer internistischen PJ Ausbildungsstation. GMS Z Med Ausbild. 2009;26(3):Doc 31. [ Google Scholar ] 19. Raes P, et al. Quality management of clinical-practical instruction for Practical Year medical students in Germany - proposal for a catalogue of criteria from the German Society of Medical Education. GMS Z Med Ausbild. 2014;31(4):Doc49. doi: 10.3205/zma000941. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 20. Bleakley A, Bligh J. Students learning from patients: let''s get real in medical education. Adv Health Sci Educ Theory Pract. 2008;13(1):89–107. doi: 10.1007/s10459-006-9028-0. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 21. Singer SJ, et al. Identifying organizational cultures that promote patient safety. Health Care Manag Rev. 2009;34(4):300–311. doi: 10.1097/HMR.0b013e3181afc10c. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] Associated Data This section collects any data citations, data availability statements, or supplementary materials included in this article. Data Availability Statement The datasets used and/or analyzed during the current study are available from the corresponding author on request.',752,'2025-10-10 08:04:05','2025-10-10 08:04:05');
INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(1322,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12420886','Challenges and Considerations in Manipulating Oral Dosage Forms in Paediatric Healthcare Settings: A Narrative Review.','Paulsson M, Hamre Svendsen R, Andersen JKN, Kälvemark Sporrong S, Andersson Y, Tho I.','2025','dispersing crushing dispersing unsupported crushing dispersing risking crushed bpca ccri edqm mamba manipulering medisiner til barn modric prea sanra smpc crushing dispersing crushing dissolving aliquot excipients hamre svendsen v25g645 prochlorperazine maleate prochlorperazine overdosing underdosing prochlorperazine prochlorperazine mesilate prochlorperazine maleate crushed crushed prochlorperazine mesilate prochlorperazine maleate crushed regained takeaway aliquot aliquot sanra bpca prea preservative crushing ccri modric modric modric adaptions mamba manipulering medisiner til barn modric modric crushed dispersing sachets immiscible syrups reconstituted edqm extemporaneous modric crushing personalisation antiepileptics cloudy undissolved excipients undissolved dispersing solubilities benzylpenicillin dissolve dissolve dissolve dissolve clemastine clemastine clemastine dissolve excipients exampe aliquoted dissolve crushing dispersing excipients turbid obscuring undissolved underdosing crushing depot crushed brustugun pharmacopoeia brustugun dispersing halves dispersions disperse stirred excipients excipient suspending immiscible dispersing deionised sparkling mesalazine smpc adsorption diluent diluent rinsing smpc crushing grinding unscored splitter splitter pulverised crushed crushing crushed crushed underdosing inefficacy crushed risked subtherapeutic vortexing excipients dispersing suspending palatability crushed bitterness crushed prochlorperazine aliquot crushed extemporaneous gelatine container dispersing extemporaneous paulsson hamre svendsen kalvemark sporrong tho nordforsk hamre svendsen tho nordin paediatrica bertsche meyrath dios errico zanon radaelli teigen bjerknes bjerknes bøyum brustugun paediatrica richey salm salm prochlorperazine maleate prochlorperazine mesilate ejhpharm richey baethge goldbeck sanra commissioner strickley eumonitor j9vvik7m1c3gyxp vkishwm1efzz ccri ccri modric qicentral medsiq modric bradnam paedform edqm kienle ch08 richey franson s41390 eksborg forberg nydert lindemalm kader liminga ljungman paulsson johannesson paulsson zahn hoerning trollmann rascher neubert vossen hassany buljac brugma vulto hanff paediatrica pharmacopoeia nydert paulsson dissolving paediatrica attwood fricker brandl brustugun notaker paetz tho bjerknes dispersing batchelor brustugun notaker paetz tho bjerknes dispersing paediatrica broadhurst nunn dispersible brustugun birkedal tho bjerknes albinsson gronlund paulsson wikstrom ahlsson paediatrica lahiq batchelor dispersible s28 s29 bjpharm martir fotaki pharmscitech nasogastrically riet nales doeve nicia splitters ciavarella faustino unscored chirehwa wiesner crushing phogole lalla decloedt kellermann crushed antituberculosis georghiou e02876 batchelor g219 a90 woerdenbag leferink reinink splitters crushers grinders lindemalm onatli eksborg forberg paediatrica gillicuddy crean sahm hashimi batchelor shokraneh bahman seoane viano trenfield basit goyanes paulsson strømme quodbach lindh tartrate semisolid','ABSTRACT Aim To explore the challenges and risks associated with the manipulation of solid oral dosage forms in paediatric healthcare. To compile recommendations to support the safety, efficacy and patient outcomes in paediatric patients. Methods A narrative review was conducted based on literature searches in PubMed, Scopus and Web of Science (2000–2024), including regulatory guidelines and clinical case reports. Selection focused on studies involving oral drug manipulation in children, particularly dose extraction, dispersing tablets, and administration via enteral tubes. Results Between 15%–37% of paediatric oral drug administrations require manipulation due to lack of age‐appropriate formulations. Common manipulations include tablet splitting, crushing or dispersing, often resulting in dose inaccuracies, variable solubility and inconsistent pharmacokinetics. Risks are elevated in children using enteral feeding tubes or when manipulating poorly soluble drugs. Studies show manipulation can lead to dosing errors with potential harm. Conclusion Oral drug manipulation in paediatrics is widespread but often unsupported by evidence. Safer practices require improved access to licensed formulations, better training and clinical support tools. Pharmacy compounding, personalised manufacturing and technologies like 3D printing may reduce risks. Regulatory alignment and interdisciplinary collaboration are critical to improving drug safety and outcomes for paediatric patients. Keywords: dose adjustments, ease of administration, enteral tubes, manipulation, medicines, paediatrics A substantial proportion of oral drug administrations in children require manipulation due to the lack of age‐appropriate formulations. Common methods such as splitting, crushing, and dispersing tablets in fluids can compromise dose accuracy because of e.g., drug solubility. This narrative review highlights key risks and proposes strategies to support safer paediatric medication practices in clinical settings. Summary. A significant proportion of paediatric oral drug administrations involve manipulation of dosage form, risking dose inaccuracies, altered pharmacokinetics, and safety concerns. Poor solubility of drug substances often leads to dose inaccuracies and inconsistent drug delivery, especially when tablets are crushed and dispersed in water and a portion given to the patient. Altering the dosage form can result in inadequate dissolution of drugs, compromising therapeutic efficacy and safety. Abbreviations API active pharmaceutical ingredients BPCA best pharmaceuticals for children act CCRI CDER center for real‐world evidence innovation CDER center for drug evaluation and research EDQM European directorate for quality of medicines and healthcare EMA European medicines agency FDA U.S. Food and Drug Administration IV Intravenous MAMBA Manipulering av medisiner til barn (Manipulation of medicines for children) MODRIC Manipulation of drugs required in children—a guide for health professionals NHS National Health Service PIP paediatric investigation plan PREA paediatrics research equity act SANRA scale for the assessment of narrative review articles SmPC summary of product characteristics WHO World Health Organisation 1. Introduction Handling drugs for children is often more complex than in settings with adults [ 1 , 2 , 3 ]. Nurses can feel that they lack the necessary skills and experience to safely handle drugs for paediatric patients [ 1 ]. Up to 31% of drug errors that occur in paediatrics have the potential to cause serious harm or even death [ 2 , 4 ]. There is a significant lack of paediatric‐approved drug products. This is due to the fact that children have historically been excluded from clinical trials resulting in a lack of information surrounding safety and effect in this population [ 5 , 6 , 7 , 8 ]. In many cases, children require active pharmaceutical ingredients (API) that are only approved for adults [ 6 ]. Consequently, drug products must be used off‐label (i.e., outside the approved market authorisation). Hence, paediatric nurses and caregivers must often turn to alternative methods, such as crushing, splitting, or dispersing a drug product in water, or mixing it with food or drink to meet the needs of the child—whether it be to acquire the prescribed child‐adjusted dose or to ease administration [ 1 , 2 ]. The term manipulation is used to describe the physical alteration of a dosage form (such as splitting or crushing a tablet, opening a capsule) often with the purpose of extracting a portion of the drug product (Figure 1 ) [ 8 ]. If the procedure involves dissolving a tablet in water and taking a portion of the resulting solution (aliquot), it requires confirmation that the entire drug content has fully dissolved in the liquid volume and formed a homogeneous mixture in the water. This can only be achieved by knowing the solubility and salt form of the API, pH of solution and excipients in the tablet, which also will be present in the mixture. FIGURE 1. Open in a new tab Examples of different methods of manipulation. Created in BioRender. Hamre Svendsen, R. (2025) https://BioRender.com/v25g645 . The clinical outcome of such manipulation can sometimes be very unfortunate. A recent case report (Table 1 ) highlights a serious adverse event for a young child involving the off‐label use of prochlorperazine maleate tablets due to the discontinuation of the licensed prochlorperazine liquid in the UK [ 9 ]. This case exemplifies the critical need for evidence‐based practices and suitable formulations to prevent such harmful occurrences in the future. Manipulation can lead to harmful events both if dose extraction leads to overdosing (toxic effects) or underdosing (lack of effect) compared to the intended dose. TABLE 1. Clinical vignette—prochlorperazine—need for understanding the pharmaceutical properties [ 9 ]. Patient: Young child with malignant brain tumour, previously stable on prochlorperazine mesilate oral liquid (licensed paediatric formulation). Clinical problem: Due to discontinuation of the licensed liquid, treatment was switched to prochlorperazine maleate tablets, which were crushed and administered off‐label. Outcome: The child experienced uncontrolled vomiting, resulting in multiple hospital visits and prolonged admission. Symptom control could not be re‐established until compensation for poor solubility and unpredictable dosing from the crushed tablets was accounted for. Pharmaceutical insight: Prochlorperazine mesilate (liquid): solubility ≈ 2000 mg/mL Prochlorperazine maleate (tablet): solubility ≈ 0.015 mg/mL ○ Large difference in solubility of the two salt forms ○ Crushed tablets did not provide a reliable dose Parent experience: ‘After the dose was adjusted, our child regained weight and energy, returned to school, and tolerated nasogastric feeds again. Vomiting became minimal.’ Key takeaway: Not all formulations are interchangeable. Substituting without understanding the pharmaceutical properties (e.g., salt form, solubility) can lead to clinical failure. Open in a new tab In a systematic review study from 2017, only limited evidence to support drug manipulation to obtain the required dose was found. Fifty studies were included, only two of these specifically investigated children, and only one study was identified where drugs were manipulated to obtain a portion of the dosage form, and that portion administered [ 10 ]. In this narrative review we compiled data on manipulations of solid oral dosage forms in paediatric healthcare. The review focuses on the handling within hospitals where a portion of the dosage form (aliquot) is taken to administer to patients. The aims were: To explore the challenges and risks associated with the manipulation of oral dosage forms in paediatric healthcare. To compile recommendations to support the safety, efficacy and patient outcomes in paediatric patients. 2. Methods Databases such as PubMed, Scopus, and Web of Science were searched using publication year (2000–2024). Older studies were also included when necessary for the context. Key search terms include (‘manipulation’, ‘aqueous solubility’, ‘oral medication’, ‘oral drug’, ‘patient safety’ and ‘aliquot’). The focus was on relevance to the topic and prioritising highly cited papers with the following exclusion criteria: non‐English publications, non‐oral drug studies and non‐primary research articles. However, regulatory guidelines and official documents from recognised authorities, such as the U.S. Food and Drug Administration (FDA), the European Medicines Agency (EMA), the National Health Service (NHS) in the UK and the World Health Organisation (WHO), were also included to provide essential context and insights into regulatory perspectives. These sources, while not peer‐reviewed, were deemed critical for understanding the regulatory framework surrounding the topic. The structure of the narrative review was developed by the first author, who aimed to organise the material based on the research questions and the most logical way to present the data. This approach was informed by the author''s clinical background from the Children''s hospital, which provided a framework for identifying key themes and structuring the content. This thematic organisation facilitated a clear and coherent analysis of the included articles. We used the SANRA (Scale for the Assessment of Narrative Review Articles) checklist to guide the development of a high‐quality narrative review. This included ensuring a clear explanation of the review''s importance and aims, a well‐structured presentation of the literature, accurate referencing, and critical appraisal of the included sources [ 11 ]. 2.1. Regulations and Guidelines Over the last 25 years, regulatory initiatives have aimed to promote the development of paediatric medicines. The European Medicines Agency (EMA) has introduced the Paediatric Regulation and Paediatric Investigation Plans (PIPs) to support research into child‐appropriate medicines [ 12 ]. In the US, the FDA implemented the Best Pharmaceuticals for Children Act (BPCA) and Paediatric Research Equity Act (PREA) with similar goals [ 13 ]. The World Health Organisation (WHO) has launched the ‘make medicines child size’ campaign to raise global awareness [ 14 ]. Despite these efforts and advancements in formulation technologies, such as a shift from preserved multi‐use liquids to preservative‐free, single‐use solid dosage forms [ 15 ], many children still lack access to suitable, age‐appropriate medicines, highlighting the need for further innovation and regulatory coordination [ 16 ]. Licensed drug products need to follow strict regulations regarding content. According to Directive 2001/83/EC, the amount of API in a drug product must not deviate more than ±5% from the label claim [ 17 ]. However, once a product is manipulated (i.e., off‐label use), whether by splitting, crushing, or extracting a portion, dose accuracy cannot be guaranteed. This applies both when only a fraction is given or when the entire modified product is used. The widespread manipulation of solid oral dosage forms is not comprehensively regulated by pharmaceutical authorities, and the extent of the problem may not be fully recognised. The U.S. Food and Drug Administration (FDA) has recently established the CDER center for real‐world evidence innovation (CCRI) to support efforts in addressing such gaps [ 18 ]. While manipulation of solid oral dosage forms is not fully regulated, guidelines have been developed in an attempt to assist caretakers and healthcare providers. The MODRIC guidelines (manipulation of drugs required in children—a guide for health professionals) from the UK are based on a large number of studies and focus on manipulations required to achieve the prescribed dose [ 19 ]. They were developed to bridge the gap between the paediatric needs, and available formulations, pending long‐term regulatory changes. MODRIC recommends avoiding manipulation whenever possible [ 19 ]. The MODRIC guidelines have become an important support for healthcare professionals beyond the UK, even generating local adaptions, such as the Norwegian MAMBA ( Manipulering av medisiner til barn ). Table 2 summarises typical manipulations across dosage forms and is based on MODRIC. The Handbook of Drug Administration via Enteral Feeding Tubes [ 20 ] is another guideline that highlight the safety considerations of altering formulations and aims to offer support for clinicians. However, these guidelines offer little to no support when aiming for extracting a portion of the dosage form, which is often the case for children. TABLE 2. A selection of dosage forms and examples of what manipulation may entail. Inspired by the MODRIC guidelines [ 19 ]. Dosage form Methods of manipulation Tablets Split/cut; a portion given Crushed; whole amount or a portion given Dispersed in water, or other liquid; whole amount or portion given Capsules Opening the capsule; dispersing the content into food or liquid; whole amount or portion given Opening the capsule and counting granules Removing the contents of a soft capsule with a needle and syringe; whole amount or portion given Sachets Dispersed in liquid; a portion given A portion of the powder dispersed in liquid Dispersed the powder in liquid when not indicated, or in a different volume or liquid than specified Drops Mixing the drops with food or drink Diluted with a liquid Dosing immiscible oil‐based drops in water (to increase volume without dilution) Mixtures/ suspensions/ syrups Reconstituted (where applicable) with a different volume of liquid than specified Diluted with a liquid Intravenous (IV) injection IV drugs given orally Open in a new tab Another valuable resource is the European Paediatric Formulary, developed by the European Directorate for the Quality of Medicines & HealthCare (EDQM). This initiative provides harmonised, validated formulations for extemporaneous preparation of paediatric medicines across Europe, supporting standardised practice where licensed products are lacking. It complements national formularies and responds to the identified need for accessible, high‐quality formulations with known stability and dosing data [ 21 ]. For parenteral formulations there are strict standards regarding safe preparation and handling, for example, in the US nurses or pharmacists preparing IV drugs with immediate use are never allowed to make as complex mixtures as adding more than two drug vial entries into a bag [ 22 ]. For oral drugs, no such guideline exists [ 23 ], and still more than 10 years after MODRIC was published children remain underserved according to a recent review article [ 24 ]. 2.2. Extent of Manipulations When it comes to how common drug manipulation is on hospital wards, there are likely significant differences between countries. In some countries, the availability of well‐developed compounding services can supply patient‐specific doses for most of the needed APIs in just hours. In others, the lack of such services is due to regulatory issues, long geographical distances, organisational issues, or economic constraints. As a result, manipulating dosage forms, such as crushing tablets, often becomes an available alternative that is a simpler and cheaper solution. Recently, a Swedish study based on a large dataset revealed that manipulations of oral drugs were needed for around 17% of administrations to paediatric patients, underlining the need for safer alternatives [ 25 ]. Another study showed that approximately 15% of oral drugs were manipulated, particularly for patients with enteral feeding tubes, raising concerns about cross‐contamination, inaccurate dosing, and suboptimal pharmacokinetics [ 26 ]. Stress among nurses due to insufficient guidance and clinicians'' lack of time to seek proper information were noted. Introducing clinical pharmacists and ready‐to‐use drugs was suggested. In a Swedish paediatric oncology ward study, 74% of APIs required personalisation due to unsuitable dosage forms or strengths. Manipulation often compromised drug solubility and dosing accuracy, highlighting the need for advancements in formulation design, manufacturing, and patient‐specific information [ 27 ]. Inappropriate manipulation of oral drugs due to unsuitable unit doses is a global challenge. For example, a 2017 Norwegian study from 2017 found that 17% of 3070 oral administrations required off‐label manipulation, primarily of tablets, due to unsuitable unit doses [ 7 ]. Poor solubility of some drugs further complicated administration. The study further underlined that standardised processes, staff training, and improved formulations are urgently needed. Similarly, in Germany, manipulation was reported in 37% of 2003 drug preparations, affecting 57% of paediatric and adolescent patients—particularly newborns (100%) and infants/toddlers (72%). Antiepileptics, vitamins, and acid‐related drugs were frequently manipulated, with over half being off‐label due to unavailable alternatives. Evidence‐based guidelines and innovative solutions are emphasised as crucial to improving safety in paediatric drug therapy [ 28 ]. A study in the Netherlands found similar results, with 37% of the administrations being manipulated for paediatric patients, with 38% of these manipulations being off‐label [ 29 ]. These data highlight the global need for more flexible and age‐appropriate formulations and underscore the importance of standardised handling practices or more on‐demand compounding with short lead times. 2.3. What Are the Risks of Manipulating Oral Medicines in Children? 2.3.1. Solubility and Dose Recovery When a tablet or capsule content is dispersed in a small amount of water, the mixture can become cloudy with visible granules at the bottom of the medicine cup. These undissolved components are excipients from the solid dosage form or undissolved API. When dispersing a tablet in water, factors affecting the solubility of the API may not always be taken into account by the healthcare professionals or caregivers. Different APIs have different solubilities. Some APIs, such as benzylpenicillin sodium, dissolve easily in water (‘very soluble’, less than 1 mL of water is needed to dissolve 1 g). Others, such as olanzapine, dissolve poorly (‘practically insoluble’, more than 10 000 mL needed to dissolve 1 g) [ 30 ]. An example from the clinic is manipulation of clemastine to administer a dose lower than one tablet. One tablet of clemastine 1 mg will be dissolved in 10 mL of water to get an intended concentration 0.1 mg/mL, but clemastine only has a solubility of 0.0004 mg/mL, which means that only a fraction of the tablet content is likely to dissolve. The actual concentration will be uncertain, and knowledge of formulation properties (e.g., salt, formulation factors) and the pH of the dissolution media is needed to evaluate this. The strength of the solution is likely much lower, leading to a potentially lower dose than intended [ 31 ]. Solubility is also dependent on the crystal form of the API used in the formulation. When a pharmaceutical company formulate oral solutions or suspensions, the particle size, excipients and the solubility of the API are taken into consideration, to achieve a safe and reproducible dosing and intestinal absorption. When for exampe a dispersed tablet is dose aliquoted, there is a risk that the API does not dissolve in the small amount of water that is used. Consequently, APIs that are poorly soluble in water may precipitate and accumulate at the bottom of a medicine cup or syringe, resulting in an inhomogeneous liquid and an insufficient dose being extracted. Additionally, processes, such as crushing tablets or capsule contents may cause the particle size, crystal form or salt form of the API to change [ 32 ]. Another issue to consider when dispersing tablets is the challenges with insoluble excipients in tablets that can cause a turbid dispersion, potentially obscuring undissolved API and affecting dose accuracy. For APIs with poor aqueous solubility, ensuring a uniform dispersion in liquid remain challenging and can lead to underdosing, if part of the liquid is administered [ 31 ]. It is of vital importance to have access to proper solubility data of the API to perform safe manipulation by extracting a partial dose. Healthcare providers may lack access to detailed solubility data or the expertise to assess an API''s suitability for this way of handling. Internet resources like DrugBank and PubChem offer valuable solubility data but often lack experimental and formulation‐specific information. Specialised knowledge is required for interpretation. Regarding the salt form of the API, the case report in Table 1 underscores the risks associated with the manipulation of drugs, particularly when solubility is a critical factor. The poor aqueous solubility significantly impacted the effectiveness of the treatment [ 9 ]. APIs that are poorly soluble and show low bioavailability are often formulated using advanced technology, so called enabling formulations, to increase the absorption in commercial products [ 33 ]. Manipulation of such drug products must be avoided as crushing, splitting or dispersion may hamper the formulation technology and radically change the solubility and bioavailability leading to uncontrolled dosing. Similarly, it is well established that tablets with a functional coating that modifies the drug release (e.g., gastro resistant, depot, sustained or delayed release) should not be split or crushed as this will hamper the intended function and lead to uncontrolled dosing. 2.3.2. Precision and Accuracy of Extracted Doses A study done by Brustugun et al. demonstrated that the ability to obtain the desired dose (dose accuracy) can be hampered when tablets are manipulated. Furthermore, the reproducibility (precision) of the acquired amount may be low depending on the manipulation and dose extraction technique chosen [ 34 ]. The study was done on four different types of aspirin tablets, with three different forms of manipulation. The results showed that the dose obtained is highly dependent on tablet formulation and manipulation technique. In some cases, an acceptable dose was obtained, but in others, the dose extracted was far below tolerable limits (down to only 3% of the prescribed amount in some instances) [ 34 ]. The study utilised a margin of ±20% of the prescribed amount based on acceptable limits for split tablets from the European Pharmacopoeia in addition to a study about manipulation of hydrocortisone tablets for children [ 30 , 35 ]. Systematic reviews confirm that Brustugun et al.''s results are consistent with other studies [ 10 ] and that uncertainty around dosing after a manipulation is a concern [ 23 ]. Previous studies investigating dose adjustment by dispersing fragments of acetylsalicylic acid tablets in water found that recovery rates varied significantly depending on the tablet type and dispersion method used. Both splitting and dispersion added to variability, and dispersion directly in the oral syringe gave the most accurate recovery for the tablets cut in halves or quarters [ 36 ]. A study from the UK found that doses from dispersions of 75 mg aspirin tablets in water were generally lower than intended, with the mean dose never exceeding 76.5% of the target. Dosing accuracy varied with dispersion time, sampling method, and syringe size, with a 1 mL syringe and a 5‐min dispersion time providing better results. To improve dosing accuracy, tablets should be left to disperse for 5 min, stirred, and doses should be measured using a 1 mL syringe. These findings highlight the need for standardised methods when preparing paediatric doses from adult tablets [ 37 ]. It is not only poorly soluble APIs that can cause deviations after manipulation of tablets. Warfarin 2.5 mg tablets contain the sodium salt of warfarin and is freely soluble [ 38 ]. Nevertheless, manipulation by splitting the tablet (before dispersion) resulted in more than 30% deviation from the intended mass. The dose fraction extracted from dispersion on whole tablets did not deviate more than 10% from the intended dose. 2.3.3. Dose Strength and Proportion of API The proportion of APIs relative to excipients in the dosage form also needs consideration. High‐dose APIs (e.g., paracetamol) constitute a large proportion of the tablet''s total weight, whereas low‐dose APIs are present in much lower proportions. To exemplify this, paracetamol tablets contain 91% API, whereas clonidine tablets contain only 0.08% API. This makes visual examination of low‐dose tablet dissolution difficult, as the appearance will be dominated by successful dissolution of the excipient constituting 99.92% of the tablet. It is likely that healthcare providers rarely reflect upon whether a tablet contains a large or a small proportion API relative to the tablet weight [ 31 ]. 2.3.4. Choice of Liquid Medium Manipulation covers a broad range of practices, from preparing simple vitamin supplements, like Vitamin D and C, to more complex adjustments such as suspending APIs with low water solubility. For instance, dilution of vitamin D oil‐based formulations in water to dose with an oral syringe, which is often seen for infants, are challenging due to oil being immiscible with water, impacting accurate dosing of the oil [ 39 ]. Using different qualities of water can lead to significant dose inaccuracies when dispersing tablets, as exemplified by a study on manipulating aspirin tablets to achieve an appropriate dose for children. Deionised water provided better accuracy than tap water, while sparkling water should be avoided entirely [ 40 ]. When it comes to choose of liquid for dispersion, the pH of the liquid is of importance. The solubility of the drugs is significantly affected by the physicochemical properties and characteristics of the liquid. As an example, it was demonstrated that the solubility of montelukast was driven by pH, fat and protein content and the solubility of mesalazine by the viscosity, osmolality and sugar content of the liquid [ 41 ]. 2.3.5. Feeding Tubes and Administration Challenges Some children require nasogastric or enteral feeding tubes. This is often a convenient way of administering medicines. A study from Germany showed that 46% of manipulations were due to administration via an enteral feeding tube [ 28 ]. It is also known that some licensed drugs, even when prepared according to the SmPC, have a high viscosity or large suspension particle size that can block narrow‐bore tubes. Further studies show that the interaction between the drug and the material of the tube needs to be considered to minimise drug loss, highlighting key factors when preparing drugs for nasogastric administration: adsorption by tube material, tube obstruction, the diluent used and the volume of diluent used for rinsing [ 42 ]. 2.4. Equipment and Material Considerations 2.4.1. Splitting of Tablets Scored tablets contain a division line or cross, and tablet splitting may be described in the Summary of Product Characteristics (SmPC). However, this does not necessarily mean that the split tablets contain equal proportions of the API. Often, the splitting is intended to ease administration rather than for accurate dose extraction. Splitting, crushing, or grinding tablets are common methods for dose adjustment, but achieving equal parts through splitting has been shown to be challenging with available devices [ 10 , 43 ]. The normal advice is to avoid splitting unscored tablets since it gives significant dose variability and should be avoided whenever possible. When splitting is necessary, scored tablets should be used, as they are designed to be divided and provide better dose uniformity. If tablet splitting is unavoidable, the use of a tablet splitter instead of splitting by hand to reduce dose variability and fragmentation [ 44 ]. In another study, it was found that provided that parents/caregivers have access to a suitable tablet splitter there is no need to specify scored tablets [ 35 ]. Pharmacists should provide clear and adequate operating instructions, specifying which tablets can be pulverised, the appropriate method and duration, and which tablets should not be crushed or split (e.g., gastro‐resistant and controlled‐release tablets). 2.4.2. Impact of Crushing on Drug Bioavailability It was also shown that the manipulation of isoniazid tablets leads to clinical failure due to a significant reduction in drug bioavailability when tablets are crushed. A pharmacokinetic study demonstrated a 42% decrease in the area under the curve for isoniazid when crushed tablets were compared to whole tablets, highlighting the potential for underdosing, therapeutic inefficacy and development of drug resistance [ 45 ]. Further, an in vitro study showed that crushed rifampicin and isoniazid administered via nasogastric tubes risked subtherapeutic plasma concentrations unless sonication and vortexing, together with the addition of excipients were done to overcome solubility problems [ 46 ]. This implies manipulation techniques that are far beyond what is easily achievable in clinical wards. 2.4.3. Practical Tools and Staff Training A study showed that children risk suboptimal dosing when caregivers (or parents) manipulate adult hydrocortisone tablets to provide the correct dose. This risk is particularly high when doses are prepared by dispersing tablets in water due to hydrocortisone''s poor solubility. Only 48% of preparations were within 10% of the target dose in a study using 10 mg hydrocortisone tablets targeting patient doses of 0.5–7.5 mg by suspending the tablet in water and extracting the required volume [ 47 ]. The role of the pharmacist is crucial as an instructor, educator, and quality guardian, leveraging their knowledge of pharmaceuticals to ensure safe and effective drug preparation and administration [ 48 ]. This also includes proper cleaning instructions to prevent cross‐contamination. 2.5. Patient Perspective An additional concern from the patient perspective is palatability, which plays a crucial role in adherence, especially in young children. When tablets are split or crushed, the protective coating designed to mask bitterness is often lost, leading to an unpleasant taste that can result in refusal or vomiting. Poor taste is a well‐known barrier to successful administration in children and may compromise therapeutic outcomes [ 49 ]. 2.6. Health Care Professional Perspective Manipulation of oral drugs is commonly performed by healthcare professionals, despite limited guidance. Both pharmacists and nurses describe feelings of uncertainty and stress, especially when manipulating doses for enteral tube administration [ 1 , 26 , 50 ]. In a survey on hydrocortisone manipulation, practice varied widely among professionals, with no clear consensus on the best method [ 35 ]. The development and implementation of decision‐support tools at the point of care can likely reduce uncertainty and support clinicians, particularly in high‐risk situations. Educational programmes and tools like decision‐support systems have shown promise in reducing inappropriate manipulation [ 51 ]. Early access to expertise in formulations and pharmaceutics can aid in development of robust clinical protocols for paediatric clinical trials [ 52 ], but formulation expertise is something that is likely lacking in many health care settings. To support clinicians, on‐demand (available within hours) pharmacy compounding, including additive manufacturing (3D printing), offers a potential in‐hospital solution by enabling rapid production of precise, patient‐specific drugs [ 53 , 54 , 55 ]. 3. Conclusions While healthcare professionals often rely on their own and colleagues'' experiential evidence, reasoning that long‐standing practices have ‘worked well,’ such approaches may obscure the risks of manipulating oral dosage forms. Although many drugs can seemingly be safely split, crushed, or dissolved when solubility permits, variability in outcomes introduces significant uncertainty. Even if these practices succeed in many cases, the potential for critical errors in the remaining cases underscores the need for standardised protocols and evidence‐based guidelines. This review has examined the critical issue of solubility and its implications in paediatric drug manipulation, as illustrated by the case of prochlorperazine formulation switch. The adverse outcomes experienced by the child in the case report highlight the dangers of using aliquot crushed tablets without adequate consideration of solubility and dose uniformity. This situation calls for urgent attention to the development and availability of age‐appropriate drug formulations. Healthcare professionals must be equipped with better guidelines and tools to ensure safe drug practices. Reflections and Recommendations From the Authors. Children are not simply small adults and should ideally have their own specific best‐practice guidelines for tablet manipulation to be applied in cases where age‐appropriate licensed drugs nor hospital compounded drugs are available options. Always use licensed drugs or well‐studied standardised, compounded preparations (e.g., from European Paediatric Formulary) when available. Using an extemporaneous preparation, such as a compounded suspension or a hard gelatine capsule with a patient‐specific dose, dispersed in water and administered as the entire volume (ensuring the container is rinsed to capture all the drug), is likely safer than using a licensed drug with a much higher dose, dispersing it in water, and measuring only part of the volume to adjust the dose. We recommend that when manipulation on the ward is required, clear documentation must be available to confirm that the process yields the desired drug dose. The dynamic nature of the situation must be considered, as different tablet manufacturers use varying formulations and salts of APIs. Additionally, factors such as variations in tap water pH should also be accounted for. A paediatric Quality Target Product Profile has been suggested, and this provides a useful tool for planning and decision‐making during the development of paediatric formulations for both new chemical entities and off‐patent drugs. (56). This tool may also be useful in defining what manipulations that can be done in a safe way. The most risk‐full manipulations, when it comes to patient safety and obtaining the correct dose, are likely those involving poorly soluble drugs, drugs with an advanced formulation technology (i.e., functional coating or so‐called enabling formulations to increase bioavailability), highly potent drugs, and situations where the child‐adjusted dose is only a small proportion of the commercially available dose strengths. Such drugs are in the greatest need of more knowledge and further studies. These drugs can be found within many therapeutic areas, but drugs used in oncology are one example. Regulatory frameworks should be strengthened to support safe manipulation practices, including harmonised guidelines. Improving the patient safety in the handling of paediatric medicines requires collaboration with nursing education institutions. Simulation labs and routine audits can identify gaps and ensure practices align with safety standards. Regular refresher courses for healthcare staff are vital to maintain high standards. Audits of hospital drug practices can identify areas for improvement and ensure alignment with policies. The implementation of clear decision‐support tools at the point of care can reduce uncertainty and support clinicians. Raising awareness and encouraging debate about paediatric drug manipulation can lead to change, aligning practices with new regulations and technology. As regulatory demands on extemporaneous preparations increase, healthcare professionals on ward level risk taking on more responsibility. On‐demand pharmacy compounding, including additive manufacturing (3D printing), offer a potential solution by enabling compounding of drugs within hours. Studies that contribute to greater knowledge surrounding administration of medicines via feeding tube, and how this impacts the dose that the patient actually receives would improve care and practices. Author Contributions M. Paulsson: conceptualization, investigation, writing – original draft, methodology, writing – review and editing, project administration, data curation, resources, formal analysis. R. Hamre Svendsen: visualization, writing – review and editing, methodology. J. K. N. Andersen: conceptualization, writing – original draft, writing – review and editing, formal analysis. S. Kälvemark Sporrong: writing – review and editing, methodology, validation. Y. Andersson: writing – review and editing, supervision, resources, methodology. I. Tho: writing – review and editing, project administration, supervision, resources, funding acquisition, methodology. Conflicts of Interest The authors declare no conflicts of interest. Funding: The authors received no specific funding for this work. However, the work was supported by the NordForsk‐funded Nordic University Hub project #85352 (Nordic POP, Patient Oriented Products). R. Hamre Svendsen and J. K. N. Andersen contributed equally to this article. Y. Andersson and I. Tho shared last‐authorship. References 1. Star K., Nordin K., Pöder U., and Edwards I. R., “Challenges of Safe Medication Practice in Paediatric Care – A Nursing Perspective,” Acta Paediatrica 102 (2013): 532–538. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 2. Niemann D., Bertsche A., Meyrath D., et al., “A Prospective Three‐Step Intervention Study to Prevent Medication Errors in Drug Handling in Paediatric Care,” Journal of Clinical Nursing 24 (2015): 101–114. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 3. de Dios J. G., López‐Pineda A., Juan G. M.‐P., et al., “Perceptions and Attitudes of Pediatricians and Families With Regard to Pediatric Medication Errors at Home,” BMC Pediatrics 23 (2023): 380. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 4. D''Errico S., Zanon M., Radaelli D., et al., “Medication Errors in Pediatrics: Proposals to Improve the Quality and Safety of Care Through Clinical Risk Management,” Frontiers in Medicine 8 (2022): 814100. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 5. Regulation ‐ 1901/2006 ‐ EN ‐ EUR‐Lex [Internet], accessed July 29, 2024, https://eur‐lex.europa.eu/eli/reg/2006/1901/oj . 6. Teigen A., Wang S., Truong B. T., and Bjerknes K., “Off‐Label and Unlice