and off‐label drugs on paediatric wards: a prospective study. Acta Paediatr. 1999;88(9):965‐968. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 19. Ross L, Wallace J, Paton J. Medication errors in a paediatric teaching hospital in the UK: five years operational experience. Arch Dis Child. 2000;83(6):492‐497. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 20. Folli HL, Poole RL, Benitz WE, Russo JC. Medication error prevention by clinical pharmacists in two children''s hospitals. Pediatrics. 1987;79(5):718‐722. [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 21. Terry DR, Solanki GA, Sinclair AG, Marriott JF, Wilson KA. Clinical significance of medication reconciliation in children admitted to a UK pediatric hospital. Pediatr Drugs. 2010;12(5):331‐337. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 22. Reece A, Hill A, Platt B, Burridge R. G171 Improving situation awareness in prescribing: A medication safety huddle – the DRUG‐gle (Druggle). Arch Dis Child. 2016;101:A89. [ Google Scholar ] 23. Bell C, Jackson J, Shore H. P3 S.a.f.e. – the positive impact of ‘druggles’ on prescribing standards and patient safety within the neonatal intensive care environment. Arch Dis Child. 2018;103:e2. [ Google Scholar ] 24. White M, Roueche A. G87 It’s a ‘druggle’: reducing drug errors and disseminating learning. Arch Dis Child. 2020;105:A28. [ Google Scholar ] 25. (PICS), P.I.C.S ., Quality Standards for the care of Critically Ill Children; 2015. 26. Society BP. Investment in UK clinical pharmacology will save and improve lives. 2021. [cited 2021 26th March]; Available from: https://www.bps.ac.uk/news-events/news/articles/2021/investing-in-uk-clinical-pharmacology-will-save-an 27. Bhoombla N, Preston J, Ainsworth J, et al. Pharmacovigilance Reports Received from Children and Young People, and Development of Information to Aid Future Reporting from this Age Group. Paediatr Drugs. 2020;22(3):335‐341. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] Associated Data This section collects any data citations, data availa