INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(1399,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8711695','Supporting consulting paediatrics in rural, remote, or vulnerable communities in British Columbia: Time for national collaboration.','Miller K, Benny A, Retallack J.','2021','underserviced bcps bcpeds bcps locums unsustainable deterrents bcps charlie rccbc rtvs charlie charlie charlie charlie hemophiliac hemarthrosis teen charlie charlie charlie terrace terrace charlie terrace charlie charlie charlie charlie spruce rccbc rccbc initiativesnetworks spruce spruce spruce spruce spruce spruce charlie spruce rccbc rccbc rccbc charlie joins familymed overdue pawlovich wensley markham carty remington greenman tina spruce spruce spruce malko huckfeldt kornelsen carthew fom crhr olt childhealthindicatorsbc botterill deshauer e502 amoafo hanbali jrh starmer frintner e20153183 tithecott innovating','Abstract Rural consultant paediatricians in Canada are in short supply. Rural communities across the country could benefit from the implementation of strategies to ensure access to consistent, local consultant paediatric care. Compared to their urban counterparts, rural paediatricians face unique challenges, including significant call requirements, potential risk of burnout and difficulty in recruitment. In response to these challenges, a number of strategies to bolster the provision of paediatric care to rural, remote, and underserved communities have evolved in British Columbia, including virtual support, the development of a rural paediatric network, and new training opportunities. It is time for discussions about the vulnerability of consulting paediatric care in rural or underserved communities to occur at the national level. National engagement will foster collaboration, drive research, and facilitate workforce planning, with the goal of ensuring that all Canadian communities have access to consulting paediatric care. Keywords: Burnout , Paediatric , Recruitment , Rural , Virtual , Workforce There is a shortage of consultant general paediatricians in rural Canada. (There is no universally accepted definition of rural. In this commentary, a rural community is understood to have a small population and/or is far away from an urban centre. Other terms somewhat related to ‘rural’ may also be used, such as ‘remote’, ‘underserved’, and ‘vulnerable’. While these terms are not identical, there is some overlap in meaning). Less than 2% of Canadian specialist physicians practice in rural settings, despite 18% of the population residing in rural areas ( 1 ). In a 2010 to 2011 survey of recent Canadian paediatric residency program graduates, 53% were practicing as general paediatricians, yet only 8% were working in rural, remote, or underserviced areas ( 2 ). Canadian rural communities could benefit from strategies to support the provision of local consultant paediatric care. British Columbia (B.C.) is geographically vast yet the majority of the population lives in the urban Vancouver area ( 3 ). Small communities face significant challenges in maintaining seamless local paediatric care. An implication of inadequate local rural health services is poorer health outcomes ( 4 ). The report Is ‘Good’, Good Enough? The Health & Well-being of Children & Youth in B.C. identified significant geographic disparities in 39 of 51 paediatric health indicators; northern and rural areas were identified as most in need ( 5 ). The BC Pediatric Society (BCPS; www.bcpeds.ca/ ) recognized the challenges faced by paediatricians in non-urban communities. BCPS surveyed its members and University of British Columbia (UBC) Pediatric Clinical Faculty in order to better understand the paediatric workforce in B.C. (unpublished data). A 22-question online survey was distributed to paediatricians in late 2019. One hundred and fifty-three of 427 paediatricians responded. Ninety-four self-identified as general paediatricians practicing outside of BC Children’s Hospital (the only paediatric hospital in B.C.) and not working as locums. These 94 respondents were evenly distributed in terms of years in practice and were dispersed among four major geographic areas (northern, interior, Vancouver Island, and within and around Vancouver). Seventy-two per cent work in communities with populations of no more than 300,000 (16% in communities of 50,000 or less and 56% in communities of 50,001 to 300,000). Thirty-six per cent of these 94 respondents plan to retire within the next 10 years ( Figure 1 ). This was true of only 23% in large communities (populations >300,000). Paediatricians in small communities (populations 50,000 or less) reported the median time spent on call to be three times that of those in large communities (populations over 300,000), at 168 hours (7 days) per month compared to 48 hours (2 days) per month ( Figure 2 ). Though there may be fewer calls in small communities, the burden of care required to manage acute or complex cases is often substantial. The challenges of patient transport and lack of access to support staff with paediatric expertise amplify the clinical burden for rural paediatricians. Simply being available at very short notice, whether or not the physician is called on to provide direct clinical care, carries an under recognized burden ( 6 ). On call work decreases the quality and quantity of sleep, can generate significant stress and requires restricting activities to those which do not interfere with the ability to work ( 6 ). Figure 1. Open in a new tab BC Pediatric Society Survey, planned years to retirement by community size. Figure 2. Open in a new tab BC Pediatric Society Survey, reported time spent on call by community size. In the 2018 Canadian Medical Association National Physician Health Survey , 30% of respondents reporting having burnout ( 7 ), which is characterized by feelings of emotional exhaustion, depersonalization and reduced personal accomplishment or efficacy ( 8 , 9 ). A 2014 systematic literature review found that one factor associated with physician burnout was longer working hours ( 9 ). While studies have not shown an increased risk of burnout among rural physicians ( 10 ), greater workload and less control over one’s schedule have been found to be related to burnout ( 11 ). Many rural communities in B.C. have paediatric consulting care provided by four or fewer paediatricians. These small groups are highly vulnerable; a personal crisis can lead to collapse of continuous paediatric care. Solo paediatricians carry a significant burden as they must arrange locum coverage or leave their community without a local paediatrician when they require time off. It is inevitable that these paediatricians sometimes defer needed breaks, resulting in an unrealistic, and potentially unsustainable, pattern of practice. These issues are likely deterrents for recruitment. Decreased interest in on call acute care has been cited as one influence for paediatric residents choosing careers in subspecialty and urban practice ( 12 ). Since over 40% of the BCPS survey respondents from small or medium-sized communities plan to retire within 10 years, ongoing recruitment, and potentially a change in the method of delivering paediatric care, is needed to simply maintain the current level of service across B.C. Some novel strategies have been developed in B.C. In July 2020, Child Health Advice in Real Time Electronically (CHARLiE; https://rccbc.ca/rtvs/charlie/ ) was launched. This 24/7 program provides rural practitioners with immediate access to a paediatrician by videoconference for case consultations. CHARLiE physicians are paediatric intensivists, paediatric emergency physicians, or community-based consultant paediatricians. Forty-seven B.C. communities have utilized CHARLiE since its launch. CHARLiE has supported a hemophiliac child with hemarthrosis, a suicidal teen from a remote First Nations community and an infant in status epilepticus in a remote ER. Feedback from rural providers has been extremely positive. One wrote “I can’t express how grateful I am as a relatively new (to) practice physician in rural B.C. to have CHARLiE”; another wrote that virtual support pathways in B.C. “will improve recruitment and retention of rural doctors, which will benefit many patients” (feedback from program evaluations). CHARLiE can also support vulnerable paediatricians (solo or new to practice) by facilitating peer-to-peer discussions, and can assist with the on call burden of rural paediatricians. CHARLiE is playing a critical role in supporting Terrace, B.C. With an extended catchment population of approximately 51,000 and located 575 km from the regional paediatric referral centre, Terrace went from having three paediatricians participating in on call duties to one. CHARLiE is now assigned as the ‘paediatrician on call’ for 1 week per month in Terrace. Even in communities able to provide seamless 24/7 paediatric on call coverage, CHARLiE could be ‘first on call’, allowing local paediatricians to be contacted only when in person paediatric care is necessary. CHARLiE could also be on call in communities with a solo paediatrician who is away, if locum coverage is not available. CHARLiE is strengthening capacity in underserved areas with virtual simulation sessions for providers. It is hoped that CHARLiE will help to reduce burnout among B.C.’s rural paediatricians. Sustaining Pediatrics in Rural and Underserved Communities (SPRUCe; https://rccbc.ca/rccbc-initiatives/rural-health-services-support-initiativesnetworks/spruce/ ) is a paediatrician-led clinical network, which also launched in 2020. SPRUCe aims to bolster paediatric care in rural and/or remote communities by providing support for the paediatricians, enabling networking, facilitating mentorship and offering rural paediatric education. SPRUCe’s first initiative is a long-term ‘metronomic’ locum program which matches groups of two to six urban paediatricians to specific rural communities. The urban paediatricians provide locum coverage for 1 week at a time, at a frequency determined by the host community paediatricians. The program supports communities with unfilled permanent positions. There are now 17 urban paediatricians providing a total of 39 weeks of locum support per year to five rural communities. SPRUCe is also developing a general paediatric outreach program for rural communities which are not supported by regional paediatricians. SPRUCe plans to connect such communities with urban paediatricians who will provide long-term outreach service. SPRUCe is engaging with UBC Paediatric Residents interested in rural practice, and is fostering connections between trainees and rural paediatricians. Both CHARLiE and SPRUCe are initiatives of the Rural Coordination Centre of BC (RCCbc, www.rccbc.ca ), a network of rural physicians working in partnership to improve the health of rural people and communities. RCCbc is funded by the Joint Standing Committee on Rural Issues, though CHARLiE is now funded by the B.C. Ministry of Health. In order to foster interest and build competency in non-urban paediatrics, the UBC Paediatrics Residency program established the Community Impact stream in 2019, available to one paediatric resident per year. This resident spends 5 months of the first year of training based in Prince George, northern B.C. (population 80,000), gaining early exposure to community consulting paediatrics. The resident also joins rural outreach clinics, and has electives with rural paediatricians. Due to its recent implementation, it is too soon to evaluate the effect of this program on recruitment to non-urban communities. Lastly, starting in 2021, the UBC Department of Family Practice Enhanced Skills Program ( https://postgrad.familymed.ubc.ca/enhanced-skills-program/training-programs/ ) offers a three month pilot paediatrics training option. The training focuses on enhancing skills in acute paediatric and neonatal care (emphasizing neonatal resuscitation and stabilization). Program graduates will gain competence that enables them to share in hospital-based paediatric on call duties in small communities. The vulnerability of consulting paediatric care in rural Canadian communities, and of the paediatricians in these communities, is an unrecognized emergency. A national discussion about this is overdue. Rural communities across the country undoubtedly share many of the challenges outlined here and creative solutions may have been implemented to support the provision of paediatric care across Canada. The B.C. programs described may provide a starting point for further conversation, as local strategies could be beneficial in other regions. We welcome collaboration with rural paediatricians from across the country and with the Canadian Paediatric Society in order to drive research and workforce planning, with the goal of ensuring all Canadian communities have access to consulting paediatric care. ACKNOWLEDGEMENTS Thanks to Dr. John Pawlovich, Dr. David Wensley, Dr. Ray Markham and Leslie Carty of the Rural Coordination Centre of BC for their support with editing this document, to Dr. Mia Remington, Dr. Janet Greenman, and Dr. Tina Webber for their support in editing specific sections of this document, and to the BC Pediatric Society for its provision of the data and data analysis for the BC Pediatric Society Workforce Survey (not published). Funding There are no funders to report for this submission. Potential Conflicts of Interest KM was Chair of the BC Pediatric Society’s Standing Committee on the Pediatric Workforce, and is Medical Co-Lead of SPRUCe (Sustaining Pediatrics in Rural and Underserved Communities). JR is a board member of the BC Pediatric Society, and is Medical Co-Lead of SPRUCe. SPRUCe is an initiative of the Rural Coordination Centre of BC. There are no other disclosures. All authors have submitted the ICMJE Form for Disclosure of Potential Conflicts of Interest. Conflicts that the editors consider relevant to 